(x+1)}=-\\frac{2}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{4}{(x+5)}+\\frac{5}{(x+1)}.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186096952 1068 #edb/ref 17592186093656 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186096952 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246842 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186096952 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186096952 1071 "Express $(-2x^{2} + 21x + 41)/((x + 1)(2 - x)(x + 3))$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186096952 1071 "Express $\\frac{-2{x}^{2}+21x+41}{(x+1)(2-x)(x+3)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186096952 1074 "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-2{x}^{2}+21x+41}{(x+1)(2-x)(x+3)} & =\\frac{A}{(x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(2-x)}+\\frac{C}{(x+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x+1)(2-x)(x+3)$ gives\n\n$$\n-2{x}^{2}+21x+41=A(2-x)(x+3)+B(x+1)(x+3)+C(x+1)(2-x).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-1$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(-1)^{2}+21(-1)+41 & =A(2-(-1))(-1+3) \\\\  & \\,+B(-1+1)(-1+3) \\\\  & +C(-1+1)(2-(-1)) \\\\ -2-21+41 & =A(6) \\\\ A & =\\frac{18}{6} \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=2$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(2)^{2}+21(2)+41 & =A(2-2)(2+3) \\\\  & \\,+B(2+1)(2+3) \\\\  & +C(2+1)(2-2) \\\\ -8+42+41 & =B(15) \\\\ B & =\\frac{75}{15} \\\\ B & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(-3)^{2}+21(-3)+41 & =A(2-(-3))(-3+3) \\\\  & \\,+B(-3+1)(-3+3) \\\\  & +C(-3+1)(2-(-3)) \\\\ -18-63+41 & =C(-10) \\\\ C & =\\frac{-40}{-10} \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{-2{x}^{2}+21x+41}{(x+1)(2-x)(x+3)}=\\frac{3}{(x+1)}+\\frac{5}{(2-x)}+\\frac{4}{(x+3)}.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186096954 1068 #edb/ref 17592186093668 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186096954 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246621 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186096954 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186096954 1071 "![](images/e1/e1b6599646532be14e417731c4a8b2f7c4dffcc475e2b99cda0279e7dbf08bc6.png)\n\nFind the area between the curve $y =-3x^{2} + 5x$, the $x$-axis and the line $x = 1$, shown above." 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186096954 1071 "![](images/e1/e1b6599646532be14e417731c4a8b2f7c4dffcc475e2b99cda0279e7dbf08bc6.png)\n\nFind the area between the curve $y=-3{x}^{2}+5x$, the $x$-axis and the line $x=1$, shown above." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186096954 1074 "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-3{x}^{2}+5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+5\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}+5\\cdot {\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-3(\\frac{1}{3}-0)+5(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186096956 1068 #edb/ref 17592186093680 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186096956 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246581 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186096956 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186096956 1071 "Express $(5x^{2} + 2x + 5)/((x^{2} + 2x + 3)(x - 1))$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186096956 1071 "Express $\\frac{5{x}^{2}+2x+5}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)(x-1)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186096956 1074 "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{x}^{2}+2x+5}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)(x-1)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)}+\\frac{C}{(x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+2x+3$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}+2x+3)(x-1)$ and get\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}+2x+5=(Ax+B)(x-1)+C({x}^{2}+2x+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1)^{2}+2(1)+5 & =(A(1)+B)(1-1)+C((1)^{2}+2(1)+3) \\\\ 5+2+5 & =6C \\\\ C & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({0}^{2})+2(0)+5 & =(A(0)+B)(0-1)+C({0}^{2}+2(0)+3) \\\\ 5 & =-B+3C \\\\ 5 & =-B+3(2) \\\\ B & =6-5 \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=2$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(2)^{2}+2(2)+5 & =(A(2)+B)(2-1)+C({2}^{2}+2(2)+3) \\\\ 29 & =2A+B+11C \\\\ 29 & =2A+(1)+11(2) \\\\ 2A & =29-23 \\\\ A & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=3,B=1,$ and $C=2.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}+2x+5}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)(x-1)}=\\frac{3x+1}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)}+\\frac{2}{(x-1)}.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186096970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186093764 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186096970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246767 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186096970 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186096970 1071 "Given that the expression $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $R>0,$ what is the value of $R?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186096970 1071 "Given that the expression $4\\sin x + 3\\cos x$ can be written as $R\\sin (x + ϕ)$ with $R > 0$, what is the value of $R$?" 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186096970 1074 "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={4}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =16+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =25 \\\\ R & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186100883 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247412 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186100898 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247395 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186100898 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247396 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186100898 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247397 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186101111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186097971 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186101111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246668 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186101111 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186101111 1071 "The coefficient of $x^{7}$ in the expansion of $(4 + x)^{9}$ is\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186101111 1071 "The coefficient of ${x}^{7}$ in the expansion of ${(4+x)}^{9}$ is" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186101111 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0