e u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,u-{v}^{2},\\,{v}^{2}⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 2$ and $0\\le v\\le 2.$ What is the missing expression?" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186120097 1071 "$∬_(D)AAAAAAAAdA, D = {(u, v): 0 \\le u \\le 2, 0 \\le v \\le 2}$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $r(u, v) = ⟨u^{2}, u - v^{2}, v^{2}⟩$ for $0 \\le u \\le 2$ and $0 \\le v \\le 2$. What is the missing expression?" 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186120097 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-2v,\\,2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2v & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2v & 2v\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2u & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2v\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2v,\\,-4uv,\\,-4uv⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨2v,\\,-4uv,\\,-4uv⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2v)^{2}+(-4uv)^{2}+(-4uv)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+16{u}^{2}{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+32{u}^{2}{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}(1+8{u}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =2v\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sqrt{{v}^{2}}=v$ since $v\\ge 0$ on $D.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2v\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124498 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247331 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124499 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247354 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124499 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247355 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124500 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247365 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124500 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247368 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124500 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247369 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124501 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247378 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124501 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247379 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124503 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247383 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124503 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247385 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124583 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247399 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124583 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247401 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124774 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121211 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186124774 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246783 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124774 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124774 1071 "Which of the following are stochastic vectors?\n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0.5\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-0.45 \\\\ 1.55\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124774 1071 "Which of the following are stochastic vectors?\n\n$u = \\begin{bmatrix}0.5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0.5\\end{bmatrix}, v = \\begin{bmatrix}-0.45 \\\\ 1.55\\end{bmatrix}, w = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186124774 1074 "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our vectors in turn.\n\n- Vector $u$ is a stochastic vector. Indeed, \n\n- its entries are all nonnegative, and\n\n- the entries add up to one:\n\n$$\n0.5+0+0.5=1\n$$\n\n- Vector $v$ is *not* a stochastic vector since it contains negative entries.\n\n- Vector $w$ is *not* a stochastic vector since the entries do not add up to one:\n\n$$\n0+0=0\\ne 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$u$ only.\"" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124776 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121223 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186124776 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246612 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124776 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124776 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.3\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- $Pq=q$\n- $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124776 1071 "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}, q = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $q$ is a stochastic vector\n2. $Pq = q$\n3. $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186124776 1074 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.8 & 0.3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1.4}{15}+\\frac{5.6}{15} \\\\ \\frac{5.6}{15}+\\frac{2.4}{15}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{15} \\\\ \\frac{8}{15}\\end{aligned}]=q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector such that $Pq=q.$ So, it's a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124777 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121229 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186124777 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246875 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124777 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124777 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $q$ is a stochastic vector\n- $Pq=q$\n- $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124777 1071 "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}, q = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the stochastic matrix $P$ and vector $q$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $q$ is a stochastic vector\n2. $Pq = q$\n3. $q$ is a steady-state vector of $P$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186124777 1074 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that $Pq=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q$ is a stochastic vector since its entries are non-negative and add up to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nPq=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0.5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+\\frac{1.5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1.5}{4}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ \\frac{5}{8}\\end{aligned}]\\ne q.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $Pq\\ne q,$ it is not a steady-state vector of $P.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124778 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121235 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186124778 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246797 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124778 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124778 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.9\\end{aligned}],\\,q=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124778 1071 "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix}, q = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$, what is the value of $a - b$?" 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186124778 1074 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{6}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{6}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{1}{7}-\\frac{6}{7}=-\\frac{5}{7}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121253 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186124781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246919 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124781 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124781 1071 "A particular town has a fleet of $720$ bicycles for rent. The table below gives the pattern of rental and return locations over a typical day.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Museum** | **Station** | **Gym** |\n| To: | **Museum** | $0.9$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n| **Station** | $0$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ |  |\n| **Gym** | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ |  |\n\nFor example, $40%$ of the bicycles at the Station are moved to the Gym every day.\n\nHow many bicycles would eventually be located at the Station if the listed probabilities remained constant over many days?" 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186124781 1071 "A particular town has a fleet of $720$ bicycles for rent. The table below gives the pattern of rental and return locations over a typical day. \n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Museum** | **Station** | **Gym** |\n| To: | **Museum** | $0.9$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n| **Station** | $0$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ |  |\n| **Gym** | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ |  |\n\nFor example, $40%$ of the bicycles at the Station are moved to the Gym every day.\n\nHow many bicycles would eventually be located at the Station if the listed probabilities remained constant over many days?" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186124781 1074 "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.9 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0.3 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & 0.2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.9 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0.3 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & 0.2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & -0.7 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & -0.8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 & 0.3 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.7 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & -0.8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-{x}_{1}+3({x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=4{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 6{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, after many days, $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the bicycles will be at the station. Therefore, the total number of bikes at the station after many days will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 720=120.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186129148 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247245 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186129150 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247247 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186129150 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247248 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186129151 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247298 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186129151 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247299 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186129153 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247300 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186129154 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247249 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186129160 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247295 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186129160 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247296 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186129309 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247362 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186129309 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247363 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186133748 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247129 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186133748 1038 #edb/ref 17592186