ue] [17592186162304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186158996 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186162304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246776 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186162304 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186162304 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}cxy,\\, & 0\\le x\\le y<1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186162304 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n$f(x, y) = {cxy,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le y < 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c$." 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186162304 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le y<1}.$\n\n![](images/01/01f3a8d99dd38ce2a728fe2cb4e4ab8de933ccd9ba27c87174779c428c303f48.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}cxy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{x}^{1}cxy\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[cx\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=x}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{cx}{2}-\\frac{c{x}^{3}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{cx}{2}-\\frac{c{x}^{3}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{c{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{c{x}^{4}}{8}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{4}-\\frac{c}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{8}=1\\,⇒\\,c=8.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186162305 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159002 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186162305 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246926 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186162305 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186162305 1071 "Let $D = [0, 2] \\cdot [0, 2]$. Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\n1. $f(x, y) = {x + y,\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n2. $g(x, y) = {-xy,\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n3. $h(x, y) = {\\frac{1}{4}xy,\\begin{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ D \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186162305 1071 "Let $D=[0,2]\\times [0,2].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\n- $f(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x+y,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n- $g(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}-xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n- $h(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186162305 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the second condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\text{ }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2y+2)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{y}^{2}+2y]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & \\ne 1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the first condition. For example,\n\n$$\ng(1,2)=-1\\cdot 2=-2≱0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $h(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}xy\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}xy\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot y]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\"" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186162306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159008 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186162306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246946 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186162306 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186162306 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}c{x}^{2}y,\\, & {x}^{2}\\le y<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186162306 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n$f(x, y) = {cx^{2}y,\\begin{vmatrix}x^{2} \\le y < 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c$." 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186162306 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\le y<1}.$\n\n![](images/b9/b9cfac7f0f82088900ab5e603588a73d4932f08f592321c797a1fd94b8bafb4e.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}c{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}c{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[c{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}c{x}^{2}(\\frac{1-{x}^{4}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}c{x}^{2}(\\frac{1-{x}^{4}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[c(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{7}}{14})]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4c}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4c}{21}=1\\,⇒\\,c=\\frac{21}{4}.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186162308 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159020 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186162308 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246646 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186162308 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186162308 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}4xy,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>\\frac{1}{2},Y>\\frac{1}{2}).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186162308 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n$f(x, y) = {4xy,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nFind $P(X > \\frac{1}{2}, Y > \\frac{1}{2})$." 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186162308 1074 "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,1]\\times [0,1],$ and the event $A={X>0.5,Y>0.5}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/7e/7e81ce16404bc6c5c826f79a88519bd6216156f5bb0f8af1eae41f19ca495de5.png)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>\\frac{1}{2},Y>\\frac{1}{2}) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}4xy\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}[{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}4xy\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{[2{x}^{2}y]}_{x=1/2}^{x=1}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{3y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{4}]}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186162310 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159032 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186162310 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246782 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186162310 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186162310 1071 "The marginal mass functions of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x, y)$, find $f(3, 1)$.\n\n| $x$$1$$2$$3$$f_{X}(x)$$0.2$$0.3$$0.5$ |  | $y$$1$$2$$f_{Y}(y)$$0.25$$0.75$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186162310 1071 "The marginal mass functions of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(3,1).$\n\n| | $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.5$ | |  | | $y$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.25$ | $0.75$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.5$ |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.25$ | $0.75$ |  |" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186162310 1074 "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our discrete random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,1) & ={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) \\\\  & =0.5\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =0.125.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186166899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247015 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186166899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247016 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186166899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247020 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186166899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247021 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186166900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247023 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186166900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247024 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186166900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247025 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186166900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247026 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186166900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247027 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186166900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247028 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186166900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247029 13194139533394