& 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186175994 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247277 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176000 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247160 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176000 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247161 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176000 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247162 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176000 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247163 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176000 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247164 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176001 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247148 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176001 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247149 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176003 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247150 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176003 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247151 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176003 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247152 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176003 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247153 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176004 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247104 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176014 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247280 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176014 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247281 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176015 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247273 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176015 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247274 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176015 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247275 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176015 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247276 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176016 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247282 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176016 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247285 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176016 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247286 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176016 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247287 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176087 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247009 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176087 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247010 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176087 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247011 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176087 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247013 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173716 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186176248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246758 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176248 1071 "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $z = 2x + 3y$ that is bounded by the constraints $0 \\le x \\le 1$ and $1 \\le y \\le 3$." 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186176248 1071 "Find the surface area of the part of the plane $z=2x+3y$ that is bounded by the constraints $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $1\\le y\\le 3.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176248 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=f(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nz=2x+3y,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 3.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+3y)=2, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+3y)=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2)^{2}+(3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4+9}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{14}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176251 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173734 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186176251 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246759 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176251 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176251 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176251 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, J = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = PJP^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P$?\n\n$x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, y = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, z = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186176251 1074 "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- one $2\\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =2,$ and\n\n- one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =2.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(2) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(2) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-2I)y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=x,\n$$\n\nand, therefore,\n\n$$\n(A-2I)^{2}y=(A-2I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173956 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186176288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246680 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176288 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176288 1071 "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533394 17592186045585 false] [17592186176288 1071 "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186176288 1074 "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 1 & 8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-