& ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & ={x}_{1}(0)=2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(0) & ={x}_{2}(0)=-1 \\\\ {y}^{″}(0) & ={x}_{3}(0)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-{y}^{″}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-{x}_{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=-1 \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{3}(0)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=2,q=-1,r=4,f(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{3},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-q-r)\\cdot f(t) & =(2-(-1)-4)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =(2+1-4)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =(-1)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247030 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246653 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247030 1069 "q-109646" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247030 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247030 1071 "The second-order differential equation ${y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+3y=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\\,y(t)={x}_{1}(t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $A.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247030 1072 #uuid "ce876e08-2e33-51ad-acbb-68581ccd0611" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247030 1074 "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=4{y}^{′}-3y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-3{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247031 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246792 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247031 1069 "q-109687" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247031 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247031 1071 "|  | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.12$ | $0.23$ |\n\nCalculate $P(X+Y=1)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247031 1072 #uuid "89e9e61a-c7f2-587b-936c-fdec6e3defa1" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247031 1074 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y=1) & =P(X=0,Y=1)+P(X=1,Y=0) \\\\  & =f(0,1)+f(1,0) \\\\  & =0.25+0.2 \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247032 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246730 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247032 1069 "q-109691" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247032 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247032 1071 "The second-order differential equation ${y}^{″}-4{t}^{3}{y}^{′}-5ty=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\\,y(t)={x}_{1}(t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $A.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247032 1072 #uuid "f94106f7-832d-58d8-9d97-c6671334800e" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247032 1074 "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=4{t}^{3}{y}^{′}+5ty.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=4{t}^{3}{x}_{2}+5t{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=5t{x}_{1}+4{t}^{3}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5t & 4{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5t & 4{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247033 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246935 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247033 1069 "q-109692" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247033 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247033 1071 "The second-order differential equation $3{y}^{″}+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{y}^{′}+y=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\\,y(t)={x}_{1}(t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $A.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247033 1072 #uuid "60079c14-f54c-51d8-9f7c-5a68a57a