7592186045585 true] [17592186247034 1071 "The third-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}-2{y}^{″}+13{y}^{′}=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & b & c\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand for $y=y(t),$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{″}={x}_{3}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247034 1072 #uuid "a885f7cd-1824-5b9b-8ab6-c996ddb18f06" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247034 1074 "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=2{y}^{″}-13{y}^{′}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=2{x}_{3}-13{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=-13{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -13 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, $a=1,b=-13,c=2,$ and $a+b+c=-10.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247035 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246584 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247035 1069 "q-109707" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247035 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247035 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface generated by the vector-valued function $r(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,u+v,\\,uv⟩$ at the point where $u=1$ and $v=1.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247035 1072 #uuid "2233f330-6910-5db5-8ede-0fc0e7814dd8" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247035 1074 "We first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,1,\\,v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}-{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2v,\\,1,\\,u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these vectors when $u=1$ and $v=1$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =⟨2(1),\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(1,1) & =⟨-2(1),\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(1,1)\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(1,1) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-4,\\,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the parameters $u=1$ and $v=1$ correspond to the point \n\n$$\n({1}^{2}-{1}^{2},\\,1+1,\\,1\\cdot 1)=(0,2,1),\n$$\n\nthe equation of the tangent plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨0,\\,-4,\\,4⟩\\cdot ⟨x,\\,y-2,\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ 0\\cdot x+(-4)\\cdot (y-2)+4\\cdot (z-1) & =0 \\\\ -4y+4z+4 & =0 \\\\ -y+z+1 & =0 \\\\ y-z & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247036 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246817 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247036 1069 "q-109710" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247036 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247036 1071 "Find the surface area of the parametric surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y=2-3u,\\,z=1+u-v\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 2$ and $-1\\le v\\le 1.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247036 1072 #uuid "84f10468-a9ec-54bf-a4d6-59a03d61dbaf" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247036 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,2-3u,\\,1+u-v⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2-3u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1+u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-3,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2-3u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1+u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,2,\\,3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨3,\\,2,\\,3⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's denote\n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nThen, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{22}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{22}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{22}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247037 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246574 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247037 1069 "q-109712" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247037 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247037 1071 "Find the surface area of the parametric surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx=u,\\,y={v}^{2},\\,z=\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3}\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $0\\le v\\le \\sqrt{3}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247037 1072 #uuid "d492e8b2-2ae8-555e-8788-3c26b7ebc400" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247037 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,{v}^{2},\\,\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3}⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(\\frac{2}{3}{v}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2v,\\,2{v}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 2{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 2v & 2{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-2{v}^{2},\\,2v⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨0,\\,-2{v}^{2},\\,2v⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-2{v}^{2})^{2}+(2v)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{4}+4{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}({v}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =2v\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sqrt{{v}^{2}}=v$ since $v\\ge 0$ on $D.$\n\nNext, let's denote\n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le \\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nThen, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2v\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2v\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}2v\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}2v\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nw={v}^{2}+1\\,⇒\\,\\text{d}w=2v\\,\\text{d}v,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}2v\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{w}\\,\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{w}^{3}}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\sqrt{{4}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(8-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246982 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247038 1069 "q-109713" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247038 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247038 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface generated by the vector-valued function $r(u,v)=⟨2u+v,\\,{v}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,uv⟩$ at the point where $u=0$ and $v=2.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247038 1072 #uuid "23625c0c-908e-5dbb-99aa-1438a2712b15" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247038 1074 "We first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2v,\\,u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these vectors when $u=0$ and $v=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(0,2) & =⟨2,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0),\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(0,2) & =⟨1,\\,2(2),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,4,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(0,2)\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(0,2) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-8,\\,2,\\,7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the parameters $u=0$ and $v=2$ correspond to the point \n\n$$\n(2(0)+2,\\,{2}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,0\\cdot 2)=(2,4,0),\n$$\n\nthe equation of the tangent plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-8,\\,2,\\,7⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-4,\\,z⟩ & =0 \\\\ (-8)\\cdot (x-2)+2\\cdot (y-4)+7\\cdot z & =0 \\\\ -8x+2y+7z+8 & =0 \\\\ 8x-2y-7z & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247039 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246824 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247039 1069 "q-109718" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247039 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247039 1071 "$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,\\text{_______},\\,\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(x,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,4].$ What is the second component of $r?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247039 1072 #uuid "ca400b5f-4070-5cc3-961a-99c6b1bfc920" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247039 1074 "![](images/56/56a380b075d10862170ed9c9a45c07dddff3eaef1ea9fb3109a61cc3d27b5ac4.png)\n\n  \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [a,b]$ has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,4].$ \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [0,4],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the second component of $r$ is $\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247040 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246749 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247040 1069 "q-10972" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247040 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247040 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{6x-25}{x(x+5)}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{(x+5)},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are real constants, calculate $A+B.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247040 1072 #uuid "fe814cd0-4d1d-5c9c-bebb-23560660b7f8" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247040 1074 "Our task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6x-25}{x(x+5)}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{(x+5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $x(x+5)$ gives\n\n$$\n6x-25=A(x+5)+B(x).\n$$\n\nFor $x=0$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(0)-25 & =A(0+5)+B(0) \\\\ -25 & =5A \\\\ A & =-\\frac{25}{5} \\\\ A & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-5$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(-5)-25 & =A(-5+5)+B(-5) \\\\ -55 & =-5B \\\\ B & =\\frac{-55}{-5} \\\\ B & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nA+B=-5+11=6.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247041 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246841 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247041 1069 "q-109721" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247041 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247041 1071 "$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,\\text{_______},\\,(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(x,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [-2,2].$ What is the second component of $r?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247041 1072 #uuid "572eb6bc-926f-5f8b-9318-f1a36bd42c44" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247041 1074 "![](images/56/56a380b075d10862170ed9c9a45c07dddff3eaef1ea9fb3109a61cc3d27b5ac4.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [a,b]$ has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(x)=4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [-2,2].$ \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [-2,2],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the second component of $r$ is $(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247042 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246734 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247042 1069 "q-109731" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247042 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247042 1071 "The vector-valued function $r(x,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=1+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}].$ Which of the following could be $r?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247042 1072 #uuid "df08515f-abd5-5351-b042-d1604c1837b8" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247042 1074 "![](images/56/56a380b075d10862170ed9c9a45c07dddff3eaef1ea9fb3109a61cc3d27b5ac4.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [a,b]$ has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(x)=1+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}].$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,(1+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(1+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247043 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246952 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247043 1069 "q