{B}{(2x+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(5-3x)(2x+3)$ gives\n\n$$\n7x-37=A(2x+3)+B(5-3x).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(-\\frac{3}{2})-37 & =A(2(-\\frac{3}{2})+3)+B(5-3(-\\frac{3}{2})) \\\\ \\frac{-21-74}{2} & =B(\\frac{10+9}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{-95}{2} & =B(\\frac{19}{2}) \\\\ B & =\\frac{-95}{19} \\\\ B & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=\\frac{5}{3}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(\\frac{5}{3})-37 & =A(2(\\frac{5}{3})+3)+B(5-3(\\frac{5}{3})) \\\\ \\frac{35-111}{3} & =A(\\frac{10+9}{3}) \\\\ A & =\\frac{-76}{19} \\\\ A & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nA+B=-4+(-5)=-4-5=-9.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247045 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246592 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247045 1069 "q-109760" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247045 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247045 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface generated by the vector-valued function $r(u,v)=⟨2{u}^{3},\\,u{v}^{2},\\,2v⟩$ at the point where $u=1$ and $v=3.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247045 1072 #uuid "5b0e017d-7cee-5467-a9a3-8897f010e06d" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247045 1074 "We first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2{u}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6{u}^{2},\\,{v}^{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2{u}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2uv,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these vectors when $u=1$ and $v=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(1,3) & =⟨6(1)^{2},\\,{3}^{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,9,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(1,3) & =⟨0,\\,2(1)(3),\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,6,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(1,3)\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(1,3) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 6 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨18,\\,-12,\\,36⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the parameters $u=1$ and $v=3$ correspond to the point \n\n$$\n(2(1)^{3},\\,1(3)^{2},\\,2(3))=(2,9,6),\n$$\n\nthe equation of the tangent plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨18,\\,-12,\\,36⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-9,\\,z-6⟩ & =0 \\\\ 18\\cdot (x-2)-12\\cdot (y-9)+36\\cdot (z-6) & =0 \\\\ 18x-12y+36z-144 & =0 \\\\ 3x-2y+6z-24 & =0 \\\\ 3x-2y+6z & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247046 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246947 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247046 1069 "q-109761" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247046 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247046 1071 "Find the surface area of the parametric surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2},\\,y=uv,\\,z=\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le 1$ and $1\\le v\\le 2.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247046 1072 #uuid "0c206356-09e7-589f-8fd6-4bbad41aebab" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247046 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,uv,\\,\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,v,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,u,\\,v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & v & 0 \\\\ 0 & u & v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}v & 0 \\\\ u & v\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & v\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2u & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{v}^{2},\\,-2uv,\\,2{u}^{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨{v}^{2},\\,-2uv,\\,2{u}^{2}⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({v}^{2})^{2}+(-2uv)^{2}+(2{u}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{v}^{4}+4{u}^{2}{v}^{2}+4{u}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({v}^{2}+2{u}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & ={v}^{2}+2{u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's denote\n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nThen, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({v}^{2}+2{u}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({v}^{2}+2{u}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[u{v}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({v}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3})\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}+\\frac{2}{3}v]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{2}^{3}+2(2)-({1}^{3}+2(1))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(9) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247047 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246897 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247047 1069 "q-109766" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247047 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247047 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface generated by the vector-valued function $r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,{u}^{3}-uv⟩$ at the point where $u=2$ and $v=6.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247047 1072 #uuid "c13e8eeb-6738-5ffa-a9d8-bdfec9424830" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247047 1074 "We first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{3}-uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,3{u}^{2}-v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{3}-uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these vectors when $u=2$ and $v=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(2,6) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,3(2)^{2}-6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,6⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(2,6) & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(2,6)\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(2,6) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the parameters $u=2$ and $v=6$ correspond to the point \n\n$$\n(2,\\,6,\\,{2}^{3}-(2)(6))=(2,6,-4),\n$$\n\nthe equation of the tangent plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-6,\\,2,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-6,\\,z+4⟩ & =0 \\\\ (-6)\\cdot (x-2)+2\\cdot (y-6)+1\\cdot (z+4) & =0 \\\\ -6x+2y+z+4 & =0 \\\\ 6x-2y-z & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247048 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246882 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247048 1069 "q-109768" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247048 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247048 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface generated by the vector-valued function $r(u,v)=⟨u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,u⟩$ at the point where $u=2$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247048 1072 #uuid "acb72faa-2c52-5364-8621-47b8f624df9d" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247048 1074 "We first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these vectors when $u=2$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-2,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the parameters $u=2$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ correspond to the point \n\n$$\n(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,2)=(0,2,2),\n$$\n\nthe equation of the tangent plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨0,\\,-2,\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x,\\,y-2,\\,z-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ 0\\cdot x+(-2)\\cdot (y-2)+2\\cdot (z-2) & =0 \\\\ -2y+2z & =0 \\\\ y-z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247049 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246821 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247049 1069 "q-109772" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247049 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247049 1071 "$$\nr(y,\\theta )=⟨{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,\\text{_______}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(y,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x={e}^{1-{y}^{2}}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2].$ What is the third component of $r?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247049 1072 #uuid "58d0905e-f0d8-507f-b32c-47b885b86fbf" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247049 1074 "![](images/be/be6fb27c0d050a7007e9f4a0d526daaff40b8f7d01f20c63ac8cc00074121f28.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $x=f(y)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta =[0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $x=f(y)={e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2].$ \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\nr(y,\\theta )=⟨{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is ${e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247050 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246996 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247050 1069 "q-109773" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247050 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247050 1071 "$$\nr(y,\\theta )=⟨\\text{_______},\\,y,\\,({y}^{3}-{y}^{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(y,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x={y}^{3}-{y}^{4}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [0,1].$ What is the first component of $r?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247050 1072 #uuid "3913185c-760e-5577-b78e-c5e6f144b52a" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247050 1074 "![](images/be/be6fb27c0d050a7007e9f4a0d526daaff40b8f7d01f20c63ac8cc00074121f28.png)\n\n  \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $x=f(y)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta =[0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $x=f(y)={y}^{3}-{y}^{4}\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [0,1].$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(y,\\theta )=⟨({y}^{3}-{y}^{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,({y}^{3}-{y}^{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [0,1],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the first component of $r$ is $({y}^{3}-{y}^{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247051 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246938 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247051 1069 "q-109776" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247051 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247051 1071 "$$\nr(y,\\theta )=⟨\\text{_______},\\,y,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(y,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡y$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ What is the first component of $r?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247051 1072 #uuid "a2e54158-a606-50ed-b02f-3e6c0856dcab" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247051 1074 "![](images/be/be6fb27c0d050a7007e9f4a0d526daaff40b8f7d01f20c63ac8cc00074121f28.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $x=f(y)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta =[0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $x=f(y)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡y\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is  \n\n$$\nr(y,\\theta )=⟨\\operatorname{csc}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the first component of $r$ is $\\operatorname{csc}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247052 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246591 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247052 1069 "q-109779" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247052 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247052 1071 "Find the surface area of the part of the parametric surface $r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,u-v⟩$ that is subject to the constraint ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 9.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247052 1072 #uuid "41677fe6-6daf-5332-9eef-d0840faca5fc" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247052 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that the region ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 9$ corresponds to the disk\n\n$$\nD={(u,v)\\,:\\,{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\le 9},\n$$\n\nof radius $3.$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}) \\\\  & =9\\pi \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247053 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246857 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247053 1069 "q-10978" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247053 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247053 1071 "![](images/09/09482b1e9903bff25b16b2239975831e6d54440a8f8ea78f5739b0eafee3d9e8.png)\n\nFind the area between the curve $y=-2{x}^{2}-x+3$, the $x$-axis and the $y$-axis, shown above." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247053 1072 #uuid "9331fa0c-5003-5d00-8a98-a52c36a44bdd" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247053 1074 "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2{x}^{2}-x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-1{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}-1\\cdot {\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{1}+3x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{1}{3}-0)-1(\\frac{1}{2}-0)+3(1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247054 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246785 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247054 1069 "q-109813" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247054 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247054 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,z=v\n$$\n\nat the point $(-2,0,\\pi ).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247054 1072 #uuid "baaf55fa-02ab-56ea-b2e6-ee65f6884f23" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247054 1074 "In vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,v⟩.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since $(-2,0,\\pi )$ lies on the surface, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-2 \\\\ u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=0 \\\\ v=\\pi \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=2 \\\\ v=\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=2$ and $v=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(2,\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(2,\\pi ) & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-2,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(2,\\pi )\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(2,\\pi ) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the tangent plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x+2,\\,y,\\,z-\\pi ⟩ & =0 \\\\ 0\\cdot (x+2)+1\\cdot y+2\\cdot (z-\\pi ) & =0 \\\\ y+2z-2\\pi  & =0 \\\\ y+2z & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247055 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246936 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247055 1069 "q-109814" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247055 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247055 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx=1-{u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,y=-v,\\,z=-u\n$$\n\nat the point $(-1,-1,1).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247055 1072 #uuid "9e710195-0e7a-5960-bf96-11e74f3e9efe" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247055 1074 "In vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨1-{u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,-v,\\,-u⟩.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since $(-1,-1,1)$ lies on the surface, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1-{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}=-1 \\\\ -v=-1 \\\\ -u=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=-1 \\\\ v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1-{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2u,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1-{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(-u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2v,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=-1$ and $v=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(-1,1) & =⟨-2(-1),\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(-1,1) & =⟨-2(1),\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(-1,1)\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(-1,1) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the tangent plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (x+1)+2\\cdot (y+1)+(-2)\\cdot (z-1) & =0 \\\\ -x+2y-2z+3 & =0 \\\\ x-2y+2z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247056 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246836 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247056 1069 "q-109815" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247056 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247056 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx={u}^{2},\\,y=2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,z=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\ge 0,\\,0\\le v<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nat the point $(1,0,1).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247056 1072 #uuid "ad085f94-6afd-50f9-a70f-781c458c161b" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247056 1074 "In vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨{u}^{2},\\,2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩,\\,u\\ge 0,\\,0\\le v<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since $(1,0,1)$ lies on the surface, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}=1 \\\\ 2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=0 \\\\ u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=1 \\\\ v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(1,0) & =⟨2(1),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(1,0) & =⟨0,\\,2(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(1,0)\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(1,0) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,0,\\,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the tangent plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-2,\\,0,\\,4⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y,\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ (-2)\\cdot (x-1)+0\\cdot y+4\\cdot (z-1) & =0 \\\\ -2x+4z-2 & =0 \\\\ -x+2z-1 & =0 \\\\ x-2z & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247057 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246566 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247057 1069 "q-109824" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247057 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247057 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent plane to the surface defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx=2{u}^{3},\\,y=u{v}^{2},\\,z=v\n$$\n\nat the point $(2,9,3).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247057 1072 #uuid "587fe7e0-6a5b-5171-8d76-0d63b7c1686a" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247057 1074 "In vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2{u}^{3},\\,u{v}^{2},\\,v⟩.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since $(2,9,3)$ lies on the surface, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{u}^{3}=2 \\\\ u{v}^{2}=9 \\\\ v=3\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=1 \\\\ v=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2{u}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6{u}^{2},\\,{v}^{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2{u}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u{v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2uv,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(1,3) & =⟨6(1)^{2},\\,{3}^{2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,9,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(1,3) & =⟨0,\\,2(1)(3),\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,6,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(1,3)\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(1,3) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 6 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}