gned}-1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -19 & -15 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-19){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-{x}_{1}-({x}_{3})=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246866 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247163 1069 "q-110657" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247163 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247163 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -15 & -1 & -15 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & a & ∗ \\\\ 0 & b & ∗ \\\\ 1 & c & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $a+c.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247163 1072 #uuid "ff4be5ca-e074-5c67-84f8-cd31721f34df" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247163 1074 "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-1)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- a single $2\\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =4,$ and\n\n- a single $1\\times 1$ block corresponding to $\\lambda =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(4) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(-1) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-4I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -15 & -1 & -15 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -15 & -5 & -15 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-4I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -15 & -5 & -15 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+15{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the first and second equation, we have ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}-1$ and ${x}_{2}=3,$ respectively.\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=0,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+c=-1+0=-1.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246893 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247164 1069 "q-110659" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247164 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247164 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 16 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & a & ∗ \\\\ 1 & b & ∗ \\\\ 2 & c & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $a+2c.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247164 1072 #uuid "254fbe1b-21b6-5069-a308-074cbe90b8d9" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247164 1074 "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- a single $2\\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =-1,$ and\n\n- a single $1\\times 1$ block corresponding to $\\lambda =2.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(2) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A+I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A+I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 16 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 16 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A+I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 16 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 16 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 24 & -12 & -12\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{3}-1).$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2[\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{3}-1)]+3{x}_{3}=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=1-2{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}({x}_{3}-1) \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+2c=-1+2\\cdot 1=1.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247165 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246679 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247165 1069 "q-110760" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247165 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247165 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $A={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:x-4y=1}$\n- $B={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:x=-2y}$\n- $C={⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}<0}$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247165 1072 #uuid "49bd226a-4547-5889-a62c-e6c2912a1520" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247165 1074 "Let's analyze each subset.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. For instance, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $x-4y=1$ since $0-4\\cdot 0=0\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x=-2y$ since $0=-2\\cdot 0.$ So $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace. No point in ${R}^{3}$ satisfies the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}<0.$ Hence, $C=∅.$ The empty set is not a subspace since it does not contain the zero vector.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247166 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246716 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247166 1069 "q-110761" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247166 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247166 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $A={⟨2t,2+t⟩\\,:\\,t\\in R}$\n- $B={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x-y=0}$\n- $C={⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=1}$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247166 1072 #uuid "fc11ea3b-3ee9-5cb9-a26d-3aab9cb82498" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247166 1074 "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. If the origin belongs to this subset, we would have, for some $t$:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 2+t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives us\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t=0 \\\\ 2+t=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the system has no solution. So, $0$ is not in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace.  It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x-y=0$ since $0-0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $y=1$ since $0\\ne 1.$ So, $0$ is not in $C.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247167 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246911 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247167 1069 "q-110762" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247167 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247167 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{3})}{{x}^{6}+1}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247167 1072 #uuid "2888c71d-c895-59c2-afdb-577636636ba4" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247167 1074 "Applying the quotient rule, using the formula for the derivative of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ and applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{3})}{{x}^{6}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{6}+1)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{3}))-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{6}+1)}{({x}^{6}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{6}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{6}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{3})\\cdot 6{x}^{5}}{({x}^{6}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{({x}^{6}+1)(3{x}^{2})}{{x}^{6}+1}-6{x}^{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{3})}{({x}^{6}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}-6{x}^{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{3})}{({x}^{6}+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246642 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247168 1069 "q-110769" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247168 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247168 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if  $f(x)=\\frac{{arctan}^{2}⁡(5x)}{{x}^{2}}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247168 1072 #uuid "3d2a50a9-fa10-517c-8b03-bc1008dc69c1" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247168 1074 "Applying the quotient rule, using the formula for the derivative of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ and applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{{arctan}^{2}⁡(5x)}{{x}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x))-(2x){arctan}^{2}⁡(5x)}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x)\\cdot \\frac{5}{1+(5x)^{2}}-(2x){arctan}^{2}⁡(5x)}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x)}{{x}^{2}(25{x}^{2}+1)}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\cdot {arctan}^{2}⁡(5x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246604 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247169 1069 "q-110777" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247169 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247169 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247169 1072 #uuid "fce053b7-4dd3-554f-bea5-f458f557cfe3" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247169 1074 "Applying the quotient rule and using the formula for the derivative of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$  we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}) & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})}{{({e}^{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x\\cdot {e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x)}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246816 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247170 1069 "q-110786" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247170 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247170 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{arccotx}{{x}^{3}}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247170 1072 #uuid "d426c97a-392b-5a03-bca2-ec79aac9b21d" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247170 1074 "Here, we let $u(x)=arccotx$ and $v(x)={x}^{3},$ and apply the quotient rule.\n\nWe use the formulae\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{and}\\,{v}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot {x}^{3}-arccotx\\cdot 3{x}^{2}}{({x}^{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}-3{x}^{2}arccotx}{{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}+3(1+{x}^{2}){x}^{2}arccotx}{1+{x}^{2}})}{{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}+3(1+{x}^{2}){x}^{2}arccotx}{{x}^{6}(1+{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x+3(1+{x}^{2})arccotx}{{x}^{4}(1+{x}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246773 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247171 1069 "q-110791" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247171 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247171 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot arccsc{x}^{2}$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247171 1072 #uuid "da94a855-f20c-58b6-8374-bcc6c7d70a89" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247171 1074 "The formula for the derivative of $arccsc$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arccsc{x}^{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule and the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arccsc{x}^{2})+arccsc{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}-1}})+arccsc{x}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =arccsc{x}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x\\sqrt{{x}^{4}-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247172 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246985 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247172 1069 "q-110810" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247172 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247172 1071 "|  | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $a$ | $2a$ | $a$ |\n| $X=1$ | $3a$ | $a$ | $2a$ |\n\nThe joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above. Find the value of $a.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247172 1072 #uuid "fa81ceca-bb91-5411-be33-3da478f81628" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247172 1074 "The support of $X$ is ${S}_{X}={0,1},$ the support of $Y$ is ${S}_{Y}={1,2,3},$ and therefore the joint support $S$ is\n\n$$\nS={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}={(0,1),(0,2),(0,3),(1,1),(1,2),(1,3)}.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a joint probability distribution $f(x,y),$ we must have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1)+f(0,2)+f(0,3)+f(1,1)+f(1,2)+f(1,3) & =1 \\\\ a+2a+a+3a+a+2a & =1 \\\\ 10a & =1 \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247173 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246868 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247173 1069 "q-110812" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247173 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247173 1071 "|  | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $a$ | $2a$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{a}{2}$ | $5a$ |\n| $X=3$ | $3a$ | $\\frac{3a}{2}$ |\n\nThe joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above. Find the value of $a.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247173 1072 #uuid "d20a10c0-d1b8-5fc4-aa27-cfad5cd3603c" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247173 1074 "The support of $X$ is ${S}_{X}={1,2,3},$ the support of $Y$ is ${S}_{Y}={0,1},$ and therefore the joint support $S$ is\n\n$$\nS={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}={(1,0),(1,1),(2,0),(2,1),(3,0),(3,1)}.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a joint probability distribution $f(x,y),$ we must have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0)+f(1,1)+f(2,0)+f(2,1)+f(3,0)+f(3,1) & =1 \\\\ a+2a+\\frac{a}{2}+5a+3a+\\frac{3a}{2} & =1 \\\\ 13a & =1 \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246698 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247174 1069 "q-110817" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247174 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247174 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡xarccot{x}^{3}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247174 1072 #uuid "7bf50188-a0f4-5cf7-996e-46360eb076ac" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247174 1074 "The formula for the derivative of $\\text{arccot}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arccotx)=-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, the formula above and the chain rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡xarccot{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arccot{x}^{3})+arccot{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{1+{({x}^{3})}^{2}})+arccot{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{arccot{x}^{3}}{x}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{1+{x}^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247175 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246993 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247175 1069 "q-110834" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247175 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247175 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{arccscx}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247175 1072 #uuid "698df1fd-5fd3-5825-8b74-4170ba6ffbf1" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247175 1074 "Here, we let $u(x)={x}^{2}+4x$ and $v(x)=arccscx,$ and apply the quotient rule.\n\nWe use the formulae\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2x+4\\,\\text{and}\\,{v}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x+4)\\cdot arccscx-({x}^{2}+4x)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}})}{{arccsc}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+4}{arccscx}+\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\cdot {arccsc}^{2}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247176 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246860 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247176 1069 "q-110850" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247176 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247176 1071 "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$  by inspection." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247176 1072 #uuid "a861accd-0de4-505d-8847-8b30f8b4e546" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247176 1074 "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{3}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=0\\cdot {a}_{1}+(-1)\\cdot {a}_{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{3}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}}.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247177 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246768 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247177 1069 "q-110853" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247177 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247177 1071 "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247177 1072 #uuid "c69f867a-804d-5cf3-bc38-00ede80f0b28" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247177 1074 "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247178 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246620 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247178 1069 "q-110856" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247178 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247178 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}c(x+y),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247178 1072 #uuid "85b1f331-9e8e-51c5-b947-1cb811c958b5" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247178 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea5ff670f03d0bffb84f0929d19b94bca62f23677f913c01e8e0949d144bc60a.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}c(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}c(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[cxy+\\frac{c{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(cx+\\frac{c}{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(cx+\\frac{c}{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{c{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{cx}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}+\\frac{c}{2} \\\\  & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc=1.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247179 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246971 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247179 1069 "q-110858" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247179 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247179 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}c{e}^{-x-y},\\, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247179 1072 #uuid "80074266-7584-5c7b-8bab-8678f0fa0b55" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247179 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0}.$\n\n![](images/99/996bd092be07e2596e30739cc4249e8ed8ef859540067a94e0f9cdd05f9e1f84.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x-y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x-y}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[-c{e}^{-x-y}]_{y=0}^{y=\\infty }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =[-c{e}^{-x}]_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc=1.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247180 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246922 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247180 1069 "q-110859" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247180 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247180 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{(1+x+y)^{3}},\\, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247180 1072 #uuid "90603bab-bd0f-5ff5-b578-328ff040206a" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247180 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0}.$\n\n![](images/99/996bd092be07e2596e30739cc4249e8ed8ef859540067a94e0f9cdd05f9e1f84.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{[-\\frac{c}{2(1+x+y)^{2}}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\infty }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{2(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{2(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-\\frac{c}{2(1+x)}]}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{2}=1\\,⇒\\,c=2.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247181 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246806 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247181 1069 "q-110862" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247181 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247181 1071 "Let $D=[0,2]\\times [1,2].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\n- $f(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n- $g(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n- $h(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-y}{6},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247181 1072 #uuid "29c9baf2-abe1-5681-94f0-fc4fef002362" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247181 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{xy}{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2y}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $g(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}g(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}g(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[y]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the first condition. For example,\n\n$$\nh(0,2)=\\frac{0-2}{6}=-\\frac{1}{3}≱0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $g$ only.\"" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247182 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246906 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247182 1069 "q-110865" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247182 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247182 1071 "Let $D=[0,2]\\times [0,1].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\n- $f(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n- $g(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x-y,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n- $h(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247182 1072 #uuid "b5dab8b1-b863-52dc-8d2a-471642421d6a" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247182 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the second condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[3x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[2{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the first condition. For example,\n\n$$\ng(0,1)=0-1=-1≱0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $h(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot y]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\"" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246881 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247183 1069 "q-110868" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247183 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247183 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y),\\, & 0\\le y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247183 1072 #uuid "b563caf4-163e-5568-8f80-cdcffafdc12c" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247183 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.$\n\n![](images/a4/a49e679b3919594edc7fe963190a754324b9b5ea980d1936121c040d0ee52284.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nW\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{[-c\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-c\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+c\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(c\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-c\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[c\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{c\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )}{2} \\\\  & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc=1.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246813 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247184 1069 "q-110931" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247184 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247184 1071 "|  | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nCalculate $P(X=1,Y=2)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247184 1072 #uuid "05b22b9b-1971-568d-aff1-a6b11e7e2676" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247184 1074 "Using the table, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=1,Y=2)=f(1,2)=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246818 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247185 1069 "q-110934" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247185 1071 "|  | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nCalculate $P(X=0,Y=3)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247185 1072 #uuid "24752c16-f19b-5da8-baa9-bb2ba1afd63a" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247185 1074 "Using the table, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=0,Y=3)=f(0,3)=\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247186 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246929 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247186 1069 "q-110935" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247186 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247186 1071 "|  | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ |\n\nCompute $P((X,Y)\\in {(1,0),(1,2),(2,1),(3,2)})$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247186 1072 #uuid "2c918d0f-d5e8-53f3-87dc-7849697dc0d0" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247186 1074 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P((X,Y)\\in {(1,0),(1,2),(2,1),(3,2)}) & =P((X,Y)=(1,0)) \\\\  & +P((X,Y)=(1,2)) \\\\  & +P((X,Y)=(2,1)) \\\\  & +P((X,Y)=(3,2)) \\\\  & =f(1,0)+f(1,2)+f(2,1)+f(3,2) \\\\  & =0.05+0.25+0.05+0.05 \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246674 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247187 1069 "q-110937" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247187 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247187 1071 "|  | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.35$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n\nCompute $P((X,Y)\\in {(3,1),(3,2),(3,3)})$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247187 1072 #uuid "93fb8ebd-88df-5ce5-b2be-8dd737a33a7f" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247187 1074 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P((X,Y)\\in {(3,1),(3,2),(3,3)}) & =P((X,Y)=(3,1)) \\\\  & +P((X,Y)=(3,2)) \\\\  & +P((X,Y)=(3,3)) \\\\  & =f(3,1)+f(3,2)+f(3,3) \\\\  & =0.05+0.05+0.1 \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246789 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247188 1069 "q-110938" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247188 1071 "The matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(A).$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2}$ are the respective columns of $A.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ \\,\\,-1 & \\,\\,-1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247188 1072 #uuid "a7d58e5a-0e86-5643-9b08-933ce7a04992" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247188 1074 "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ we need to check if the equation $Ax=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & \\,\\,-4 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,\\,-2 & 2 \\\\ \\,\\,-1 & \\,\\,-1 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & \\,\\,-4 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & \\,\\,-4 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=-6 \\\\ 0=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the false statement $0=1.$ \n\nTherefore, the system is inconsistent, which implies that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(A).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246766 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247189 1069 "q-110955" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247189 1071 "The matrix $M$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(M).$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{m}_{1}+{x}_{2}{m}_{2},$ and ${m}_{1},$  ${m}_{2}$ are the respective columns of $M.$ \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247189 1072 #uuid "e29736ad-5a46-5b75-954a-5f47e0d7b1bc" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247189 1074 "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(M),$ we need to check if the equation $Mx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[M\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[M\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ Substituting into the first equation gives  ${x}_{1}=6.$  \n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Mx=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(M).$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}=6\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{m}_{1}}+(-2)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{m}_{2}}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=6(-2)=-12.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246948 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247190 1069 "q-110964" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247190 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247190 1071 "|  | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.01$ | $0.04$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.04$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.04$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.02$ | $0.04$ | $0.01$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCalculate $P(Y=2X)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247190 1072 #uuid "63d987fb-499a-5b52-8a69-e6197d5448b9" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247190 1074 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y=2X) & =P(X=0,Y=0)+P(X=1,Y=2)+P(X=2,Y=4) \\\\  & =f(0,0)+f(1,2)+f(2,4) \\\\  & =0.1+0.2+0.2 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246898 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247191 1069 "q-110969" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247191 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247191 1071 "The matrix $C$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(C).$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2}$ are the respective columns of $C.$\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247191 1072 #uuid "7005444a-a247-5177-8b52-a944d472324a" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247191 1074 "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(C),$ we need to check if the equation $Cx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[C\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[C\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 9 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=9 \\\\ 0=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get the false statement $0=12.$  \n\nTherefore, the system is inconsistent, which implies that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(C).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246709 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247192 1069 "q-110972" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247192 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247192 1071 "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ ${a}_{3},$ and ${a}_{4}$ are given below. Determine whether $B={{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3},$ where ${a}_{4}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{4}=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -29 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247192 1072 #uuid "596db895-6072-51ec-b248-ca9f56bec30c" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247192 1074 "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -29 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=-9 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=-29 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 6 & -29 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=-1,$ ${x}_{2}=-2,$ and ${x}_{3}=-3,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+(-2)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+(-3)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -29 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=(-1)+(-2)+(-3)=-6.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246939 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247193 1069 "q-110974" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247193 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247193 1071 "Consider the matrix given by $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(A).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247193 1072 #uuid "c4554bbb-8fca-5283-8d92-22082ac865b7" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247193 1074 "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ then the equation $Ax=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -2 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & k-2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ 0=k-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k-10,$ which means that $k=10.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247194 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246712 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247194 1069 "q-110976" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247194 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247194 1071 "Consider the matrix given by $L=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}7k-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(L).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247194 1072 #uuid "48a49394-e77c-53ab-a102-d1b40a287f43" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247194 1074 "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(L),$ then the equation $Lx=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[L\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[L\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -1 & 7k-1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -1 & 7k-1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 8 & -1 & 7k-1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-8{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 7k-25\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 7k-25\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+9{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7k-7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ {x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 0=7k-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=7k-7,$ which means that $k=1.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246787 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247195 1069 "q-110977" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247195 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}c{e}^{x-y},\\, & 0\\le x<2y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247195 1072 #uuid "e5fb35e0-0098-50b2-92bd-fb03dcef42bb" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247195 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x<2y\\le 2}.$\n\n![](images/60/60acb4126a456e51e00db8bbeb2f8dbc6a773a4792dda7838d2aa6a3553fa739.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}c{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}c{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating  $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}c{e}^{x-y}\\,dx\\,dy & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-y}[{\\int }_{0}^{2y}{e}^{x}\\,dx]dy \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-y}[{e}^{x}]_{x=0}^{x=2y}\\,dy \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-y}({e}^{2y}-{e}^{0})dy \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{y}-{e}^{-y})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{y}-{e}^{-y})\\,dy & =c[{e}^{y}+{e}^{-y}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =c({e}^{1}+{e}^{-1})-({e}^{0}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =c(e+{e}^{-1}-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc(e+{e}^{-1}-2)=1\\,⇒\\,c=\\frac{1}{e+{e}^{-1}-2}.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247196 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246861 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247196 1069 "q-110979" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247196 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247196 1071 "Consider the matrix given by $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ k \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(A).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247196 1072 #uuid "fc664822-35fb-5c04-9e53-89dd85c50706" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247196 1074 "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ then the equation $Ax=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -3 & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -3 & k \\\\ -5 & 1 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -3 & k \\\\ 0 & 6 & 42\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -3 & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 42+2k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=10 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}=k \\\\ 0=42+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=42+2k,$ which means that $k=-21.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247197 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246625 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247197 1069 "q-110982" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247197 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247197 1071 "The matrix $C$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(C).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}{c}_{3},$ and ${c}_{1}$, ${c}_{2}$, and ${c}_{3}$ are the respective columns of $C.$\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247197 1072 #uuid "b861c0c2-25f8-5f54-84a3-3dea6f60c30d" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247197 1074 "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(C),$ we need to check if the equation $Cx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[C\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[C\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 & -12 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 & -12 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 & -14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 & -12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=-8 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=-12 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting it into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-(2) & =-12 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-8-3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =-8-3(-5)-3(2) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Cx=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(C).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}=1\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{1}}+(-5)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{2}}+2\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=1\\cdot (-5)\\cdot 2=-10.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247198 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246670 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247198 1069 "q-110983" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247198 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247198 1071 "The matrix $M$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(M).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{m}_{1}+{x}_{2}{m}_{2}+{x}_{3}{m}_{3},$ and ${m}_{1}$, ${m}_{2}$, and ${m}_{3}$ are the respective columns of $M.$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247198 1072 #uuid "85af463a-fe91-5600-aaed-1c1e9aea31cb" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247198 1074 "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(M),$ we need to check if the equation $Mx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[M\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[M\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 & -24 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 4 & -24\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=9 \\\\ {x}_{2}=8 \\\\ {x}_{3}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+(8) & =9 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Mx=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(M).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${m}_{1},$ ${m}_{2},$ and ${m}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}=1\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{m}_{1}}+8\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{m}_{2}}+2\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{m}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=1\\cdot 8\\cdot 2=16.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247199 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246751 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247199 1069 "q-110985" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247199 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247199 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}c(1-xy),\\, & {y}^{2}\\le x\\le y, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247199 1072 #uuid "bf819c75-7419-5272-b743-a86ae3746c07" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247199 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le y}.$\n\n![](images/1e/1ef0c7b289e24a98366c64b9a213c4f67d2fc63157b3319f0118eb16c514097e.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y}c(1-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating  $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y}c(1-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[cx-\\frac{cy{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x={y}^{2}}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-({y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2}))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}c(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2})\\,dy & =c{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{8}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{6}}{12}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}-\\frac{c}{8}-\\frac{c}{3}+\\frac{c}{12}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{8}=1\\,⇒\\,c=8.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247200 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246871 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247200 1069 "q-110986" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247200 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247200 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y},\\, & 1\\le x\\le e,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{x}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247200 1072 #uuid "800aa4d6-ad4a-5a10-9d04-aca005ce255a" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247200 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{x}}.$\n\n![](images/26/268e469b0306d22e6b6dc05905a9d56a1cabd4b0e405f741ff458ca241440d4a.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{c}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx=1.\n$$\n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{c}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{c}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c}{x}[{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c}{x}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)]_{y=e}^{y={e}^{x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx & ={[\\frac{c{ln}^{2}⁡x}{2}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{c{ln}^{2}⁡e}{2}-\\frac{c{ln}^{2}⁡1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{2}=1\\,⇒\\,c=2.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246660 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247201 1069 "q-110987" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247201 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247201 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}cx{e}^{y},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247201 1072 #uuid "f09c6e4e-1ed7-59e2-b591-ad3f2f59a93f" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247201 1074 "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x}.$\n\n![](images/8a/8a94024cbce0389b1d9c60bf64a38cb7c8e8e52836b2b39e9e638f856a1c5269.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}cx{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}cx{e}^{y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}cx[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{e}^{y}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}cx[{e}^{y}]_{y=0}^{y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}cx({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}-{e}^{0})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}cx(x-1)dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(c{x}^{2}-cx)dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(c{x}^{2}-cx)dx & ={[\\frac{c{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{c{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{8c}{3}-\\frac{4c}{2})-(\\frac{c}{3}-\\frac{c}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5c}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5c}{6}=1\\,⇒\\,c=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246688 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247202 1069 "q-111032" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247202 1071 "|  | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nCompute $P(X=2)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247202 1072 #uuid "6466b906-2338-59f9-b490-f79a50950cf6" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247202 1074 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =P((X,Y)\\in {(2,2),(2,3)}) \\\\  & =P((X,Y)=(2,2))+P((X,Y)=(2,3)) \\\\  & =f(2,2)+f(2,3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246656 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247203 1069 "q-111041" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247203 1071 "|  | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=5$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n\nCompute $P(X>2\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\text{ }Y=1)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247203 1072 #uuid "7d3b29f7-b89d-5cca-95e8-db9e87534a79" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247203 1074 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\text{ }Y=1) & =P((X,Y)\\in {(3,1),(4,1),(5,1)}) \\\\  & =P((X,Y)=(3,1))+P((X,Y)=(4,1))+P((X,Y)=(5,1)) \\\\  & =f(3,1)+f(4,1)+f(5,1) \\\\  & =0.05+0.1+0.15 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246742 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247204 1069 "q-111050" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247204 1071 "The matrix $E$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(E).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}\\cdot {x}_{4}$, where ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, ${x}_{3}$, and ${x}_{4}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{e}_{1}+{x}_{2}{e}_{2}+{x}_{3}{e}_{3}+{x}_{4}{e}_{4},$ and ${e}_{1}$, ${e}_{2}$, ${e}_{3}$, and ${e}_{4}$ are respective columns of $E.$\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -15 & 0 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247204 1072 #uuid "e1365590-672c-5168-b364-de16fc710406" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247204 1074 "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(E),$ we need to check if the equation $Ex=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[E\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -15 & 0 & 20 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 & -1 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 & -1 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+4{x}_{4}=-2 \\\\ 0=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}+7{x}_{3}-{x}_{4}=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get a false statement $0=2.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(E).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247205 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246701 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247205 1069 "q-111053" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247205 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247205 1071 "The matrix $M$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(M).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}\\cdot {x}_{4}$, where ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, ${x}_{3}$, and ${x}_{4}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{m}_{1}+{x}_{2}{m}_{2}+{x}_{3}{m}_{3}+{x}_{4}{m}_{4},$ and ${m}_{1}$, ${m}_{2}$, ${m}_{3}$, and ${m}_{4}$ are respective columns of $M.$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -3 & 6 & -12\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247205 1072 #uuid "31975565-2751-59e9-bd8b-c536e60e66d3" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247205 1074 "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(M),$ we need to check if the equation $Mx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[M\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[M\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -3 & 6 & -12 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}+4{x}_{4}=-3 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-{x}_{4}=1 \\\\ 0=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get a false statement $0=8.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(M).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246932 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247206 1069 "q-111076" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247206 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247206 1071 "|  | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=4$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ |\n\nCompute $P(X=4\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y>4)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247206 1072 #uuid "ed526ede-6ba9-5979-b07c-e565cdc8a6e2" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247206 1074 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y>4) & =P((X,Y)\\in {(2,5),(4,1),(4,3),(4,5)}) \\\\  & =P((X,Y)=(2,5))+P((X,Y)=(4,1)) \\\\  & +P((X,Y)=(4,3))+P((X,Y)=(4,5)) \\\\  & =f(2,5)+f(4,1)+f(4,3)+f(4,5) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247207 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246837 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247207 1069 "q-111079" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247207 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247207 1071 "The matrix $A$ and vectors ${b}_{1}$, ${b}_{2}$, ${b}_{3}$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $\\text{Null}(A)?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247207 1072 #uuid "1fc4bebd-83f9-51c8-a49f-5447951d22bc" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247207 1074 "A vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A)$ if $Ab=0.$ So, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{b}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\\,✓ \\\\ A{b}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{3}$ lie in $\\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246870 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247208 1069 "q-111080" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247208 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247208 1071 "|  | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ |\n\nCompute $P(X<3\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y>2)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247208 1072 #uuid "74ac467f-63a1-57b7-8a81-dffcf77d58c6" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247208 1074 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y>2) & =P((X,Y)\\in {(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,3)}) \\\\  & =P((X,Y)=(2,1))+P((X,Y)=(2,2)) \\\\  & +P((X,Y)=(2,3))+P((X,Y)=(3,3)) \\\\  & =f(2,1)+f(2,2)+f(2,3)+f(3,3) \\\\  & =0.1+0.15+0.3+0.15 \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247209 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246731 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247209 1069 "q-111081" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247209 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247209 1071 "|  | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ | $0.06$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.04$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n\nCompute $P(X>1\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y=0)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247209 1072 #uuid "7b0070a7-d639-52f6-9076-6af2ae611dd0" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247209 1074 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>1\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y=0) & =P((X,Y)\\in {(0,0),(1,0),(2,0),(2,1),(2,2)}) \\\\  & =P((X,Y)=(0,0))+P((X,Y)=(1,0))+P((X,Y)=(2,0)) \\\\  & +P((X,Y)=(2,1))+P((X,Y)=(2,2)) \\\\  & =f(0,0)+f(1,0)+f(2,0)+f(2,1)+f(2,2) \\\\  & =0.15+0.1+0.05+0.1+0.1 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247210 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246889 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247210 1069 "q-111083" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247210 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247210 1071 "|  | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n\nCompute $P(X=1\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y<2)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247210 1072 #uuid "0172d82e-e716-5982-89be-170e2ffe9720" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247210 1074 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y<2) & =P((X,Y)\\in {(0,1),(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)}) \\\\  & =P((X,Y)=(0,1))+P((X,Y)=(1,1)) \\\\  & +P((X,Y)=(1,2))+P((X,Y)=(2,1)) \\\\  & =f(0,1)+f(1,1)+f(1,2)+f(2,1) \\\\  & =0.1+0.25+0.1+0.25 \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247211 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246606 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247211 1069 "q-111091" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247211 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247211 1071 "The matrix $A$ and vectors ${b}_{1}$, ${b}_{2}$, ${b}_{3}$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $\\text{Null}(A)?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247211 1072 #uuid "6241d446-37e4-5ee6-bee7-f843f3fd7347" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247211 1074 "A vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A)$ if $Ab=0.$ So, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{b}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}39 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}34 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-33 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, none of the vectors lies in $\\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246628 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247212 1069 "q-111106" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247212 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247212 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2k \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $k$ such that $b\\in \\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247212 1072 #uuid "91993936-f250-5744-8e70-dfa072854477" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247212 1074 "If $b\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $Ab=0.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ab & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2k \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2k-4 \\\\ 8k+16\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2k-4=0 \\\\ 8k+16=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=-2.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246707 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247213 1069 "q-111109" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247213 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 15 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $k$ such that $b\\in \\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247213 1072 #uuid "a032c19b-0ca4-5a55-a84b-ea09e4ff9a45" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247213 1074 "If $b\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $Ab=0.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ab & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-5 & 15 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-5k+60 \\\\ k-12\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-5k+60=0 \\\\ k-12=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=12.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246934 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247214 1069 "q-111112" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247214 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}28 & -7 & 14 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ k \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $k$ such that $b\\in \\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247214 1072 #uuid "6e9b223d-2f2b-5f4b-84d9-fe17b583795e" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247214 1074 "If $b\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $Ab=0.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ab & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}28 & -7 & 14 \\\\ -4 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ k \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-7k+14 \\\\ k-2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-7k+14=0 \\\\ k-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=2.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247215 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246729 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247215 1069 "q-111150" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247215 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247215 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{xy}{4},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X<Y).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247215 1072 #uuid "9ffe233c-16f5-535b-b931-9450db9f86d9" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247215 1074 "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,2]\\times [0,2],$ and the event $A={X<Y}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/92/9213a4b132f625677fcc5719e71900d037ac08088dc477f8d6a93f9ca67bc3d4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<Y) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{xy}{4}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{xy}{4}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{8}]}_{x=0}^{x=y}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{8}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{32}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247216 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246725 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247216 1069 "q-111153" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247216 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247216 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x-y},\\, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X+Y<1).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247216 1072 #uuid "37806d5b-467c-5ceb-9e6a-e02e1e4cdd62" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247216 1074 "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,\\infty )\\times [0,\\infty ),$ and the event $A={X+Y<1}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/df/df0bf32b4785a9f43946bd3b08f1b255549485f2275c74607fb5285e2b1baa3c.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y<1) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-y}{e}^{-x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-y}{e}^{-x-y}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[-{e}^{-x-y}]_{x=0}^{x=1-y}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{-y}-{e}^{-1})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[-{e}^{-y}-{e}^{-1}y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1}-{e}^{-1}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{e-2}{e} \\\\  & ≈0.264.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247217 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246958 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247217 1069 "q-111162" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247217 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247217 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y),\\, & 0\\le x\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247217 1072 #uuid "70065778-db0a-5680-ada4-1a1c95f48e99" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247217 1074 "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}},$ and the event $A={X\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/ea/ea585fb6e12672f5cc854578cb12ff0357cfcd8d2f3d3b75a82723241cdaf3d4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{x}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[{\\int }_{x}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]_{y=x}^{y=\\pi /2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3-\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & ≈0.793.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247218 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246616 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247218 1069 "q-111163" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247218 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247218 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{(1+x+y)^{3}},\\, & 0\\le y\\le x, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X+Y<2).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247218 1072 #uuid "8656cfbb-6643-57f2-9ab0-17cded63f444" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247218 1074 "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D={0\\le y\\le x},$ and the event $A={X+Y<2}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/f7/f7a0f0fb9d20a4b88d0aa4c395e2adb1d62195601ce756f57a48becc6ab38681.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y<2) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}\\frac{4}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}\\frac{4}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{2}{(1+x+y)^{2}}]}_{x=y}^{x=2-y}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{2}{(1+2y)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{9})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{1+2y}-\\frac{2y}{9}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{9}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246992 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247219 1069 "q-111167" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247219 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247219 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X+Y>1).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247219 1072 #uuid "5199c42f-aff1-55e5-b62b-a83ffcaa9370" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247219 1074 "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,1]\\times [0,1],$ and the event $A={X+Y>1}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/58/58c1beb1b13680e330260d93fdf8e60460e2bfeae4c1cbbc2fce8b261afe7ea1.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>1) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{A}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(A) \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246972 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247220 1069 "q-111168" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247220 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247220 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>1).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247220 1072 #uuid "bdc40edd-ea5e-57db-9e7a-f82ff5703a98" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247220 1074 "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,2]\\times [0,1],$ and the event $A={X>1}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/26/26cbd44827c3a5a3fde5d61b631ce4f214bcf9a36ce6bf76129b1fe9f9478eff.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>1) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{A}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{Area}(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246805 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247221 1069 "q-111178" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247221 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247221 1071 "Given the matrix \n$G=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(G).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247221 1072 #uuid "0d334943-5066-50df-84ae-8536ac5ce55d" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247221 1074 "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(G),$ we reduce the matrix $G$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${g}_{1}$ and ${g}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(G)$ is ${{g}_{1},{g}_{3}},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246953 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247222 1069 "q-111181" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247222 1071 "Given the matrix \n$F=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 12 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(F).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247222 1072 #uuid "b29a9ac7-6295-5ca9-9834-73c9624fb42e" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247222 1074 "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(F),$ we reduce the matrix $F$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 12 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${f}_{1}$ and ${f}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is ${{f}_{1},{f}_{2}},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246598 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247223 1069 "q-111220" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247223 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ Find $\\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247223 1072 #uuid "6d07bd1b-dc0b-5c96-a400-d7f17ccf0d9a" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247223 1074 "We need to solve the equation $Ax=0.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second equation tells us that ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+3(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the only solution to this system.\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Null}(A)={0}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246877 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247224 1069 "q-111385" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247224 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $4,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P+Q$ is always a polynomial\n- $P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $3$\n- $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247224 1072 #uuid "bc384b99-060e-5785-8885-6518d3e3cae1" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247224 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}$ and ${x}^{4},$ respectively. Since there is no ${x}^{4}$ term in $P$ that could cancel the ${x}^{4}$ term in $Q,$ the leading term in $P-Q$  still contains ${x}^{4}.$  Therefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must always be $4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For the cases where $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is a polynomial, we need that the degree of $P$ should be greater or equal to the degree of $Q,$ which is not the case.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246654 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247225 1069 "q-111390" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247225 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P\\cdot Q$ is a polynomial of degree $4$\n- $P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $1$\n- $P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $2$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247225 1072 #uuid "5d80a52b-66a2-53ce-b5ad-ecebb8222def" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247225 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must both contain ${x}^{2}.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{2+2}={x}^{4},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $4.$\n\n- Statement II is true. For example, suppose that $P={x}^{2}+x$ and $Q={x}^{2}.$ Then, $P-Q={x}^{2}+x-{x}^{2}=x,$ which is a polynomial of degree $1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{2}$ and $Q=-{x}^{2}.$ Then, $P+Q={x}^{2}+(-{x}^{2})=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $2.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246974 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247226 1069 "q-111465" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247226 1071 "Find a basis of the null space of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247226 1072 #uuid "dfae24bc-3382-50d4-8245-f369825a9256" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247226 1074 "To find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $Ax=0$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$\n\nThe given matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. So, the equation $Ax=0$ corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=-3{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{2}=6{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, ${x}_{1}$ and  ${x}_{2}$ are basic variables corresponding to the pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd), and ${x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are free variable corresponding to the non-pivot columns. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{4} \\\\ 6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4}\\in R.$ This implies that\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate the $\\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246595 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247227 1069 "q-111468" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247227 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247227 1071 "The matrix $D$ is given below. Let ${d}_{1}$, ${d}_{2}$, and ${d}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $D.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{d}_{1},{d}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(D).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${d}_{2}={x}_{1}{d}_{1}+{x}_{3}{d}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 24 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247227 1072 #uuid "1560ce17-f00a-5fcc-81e8-d48fb2971bfa" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247227 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 24 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{16}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$ is ${{d}_{1},{d}_{3}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${d}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{2}}={{(-6)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{1}}+{{(0)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=-6\\cdot 0=0.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246894 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247228 1069 "q-111502" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247228 1071 "The matrix $T$ is given below. Let ${t}_{1}$, ${t}_{2}$, and ${t}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $T.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{t}_{1},{t}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(T).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${t}_{3}={x}_{1}{t}_{1}+{x}_{2}{t}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 30\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247228 1072 #uuid "4dc6d34c-bae9-5213-a501-60bff181fa06" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247228 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $T$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 30\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)$ is ${{t}_{1},{t}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${t}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{t}_{3}}={{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{t}_{1}}+{{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{t}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 3=6.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246673 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247229 1069 "q-111503" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247229 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247229 1071 "The matrix $T$ is given below. Let ${t}_{1}$, ${t}_{2}$, and ${t}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $T.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{t}_{1},{t}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(T).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${t}_{2}={x}_{1}{t}_{1}+{x}_{3}{t}_{3}.$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247229 1072 #uuid "6370c4cf-a79d-5976-a99d-14f57fd13b52" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247229 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $T$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)$ is ${{t}_{1},{t}_{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\text{{}{t}_{1},{t}_{3}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(T).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246800 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247230 1069 "q-111507" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247230 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247230 1071 "The matrices $A$ and $B$ are given below. Given that $A$ is row equivalent to $B$, then find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247230 1072 #uuid "e9612706-eaed-52ed-a082-8ce2b4013465" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247230 1074 "To find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $Ax=0$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$\n\nSince $A∼B,$ the equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the equation $Bx=0,$ which corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{2}={x}_{4},$  and ${x}_{3}=0,$ where  ${x}_{4}$ is a free variable. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,{x}_{4}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247231 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246662 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247231 1069 "q-111508" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247231 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247231 1071 "The matrix $T$ is given below. Let ${t}_{1}$, ${t}_{2}$, and ${t}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $T.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{t}_{1},{t}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(T).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${t}_{3}={x}_{1}{t}_{1}+{x}_{2}{t}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247231 1072 #uuid "a6f035c1-4a90-5ef7-aa00-642c727bf0f8" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247231 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $T$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{12}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)$ is ${{t}_{1},{t}_{3}}.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\text{{}{t}_{1},{t}_{2}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(T).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246927 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247232 1069 "q-111509" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247232 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247232 1071 "Given the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$, find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247232 1072 #uuid "dc0b0ced-e72a-5ee0-ae93-04a4f0845d7c" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247232 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}=0,$ where ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246977 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247233 1069 "q-111514" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247233 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247233 1071 "The matrix $C$ is given below. Let ${c}_{1}$, ${c}_{2}$,  ${c}_{3}$, and ${c}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $C.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(C).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${c}_{4}={x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -4 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247233 1072 #uuid "a52283aa-8e43-5623-8f72-5c841a8ea5a0" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247233 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $C$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -4 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -12 \\\\ 3 & -4 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -12 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(C)$ is ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${c}_{4}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{4}}={{(4)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{1}}+{{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=4\\cdot 3=12.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246721 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247234 1069 "q-111516" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247234 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247234 1071 "The matrix $R$ is given below. Let ${r}_{1}$, ${r}_{2}$,  ${r}_{3}$ and ${r}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $R.$\nDetermine if \n$B=\\text{{}{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(R).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${r}_{4}={x}_{1}{r}_{1}+{x}_{2}{r}_{2}+{x}_{3}{r}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 12 & -6 & 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247234 1072 #uuid "840e6c59-db8d-561a-a36d-d9c509886863" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247234 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $R$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 12 & -6 & 6 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -6 & 6 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-12){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -6 & -18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -6 & -18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(R)$ is ${{r}_{1},{r}_{2}}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B={{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(R).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246853 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247235 1069 "q-11154" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247235 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247235 1071 "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1-{n}^{4}}{3{n}^{2}+5}$ for $n\\ge 1?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247235 1072 #uuid "0cf1b903-1513-5e37-b092-1ae17c7691a5" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247235 1074 "The dominant term  in the denominator is $3{n}^{2}$, so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-{n}^{4}}{3{n}^{2}+5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{{n}^{4}}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{3{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}-{n}^{2})}{(3+\\frac{5}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{0-{n}^{2}}{3+0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{{n}^{2}}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246887 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247236 1069 "q-111582" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247236 1071 "Find the least common multiple of $2(z-4)^{2}$ and $7(z-4)^{2}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247236 1072 #uuid "1022040b-f64c-5ed6-9959-011542bf6e8b" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247236 1074 "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(2,7):$\n\n- Multiples of $2:$ $\\,2,\\,4,…,\\,12,\\,14,16,…$\n\n- Multiples of $7:$ $\\,7,\\,14,\\,21,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(2,7)=14.$\n\nSince the variable part is the same in the expressions, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((z-4)^{2},\\,(z-4)^{2})=(z-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2(z-4)^{2},\\,7(z-4)^{2})=14\\cdot (z-4)^{2}=14(z-4)^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246608 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247237 1069 "q-111597" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247237 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247237 1071 "Find the least common multiple of the polynomials $4(z+2)^{2}(w-5)^{2}$ and $7(z+2)^{3}(w-5)^{4}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247237 1072 #uuid "833262c0-3393-5535-bbbd-ce39cdbc03d9" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247237 1074 "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(4,7):$\n\n- Multiples of $4:$ $\\,4,\\,8,\\,12,\\,16,\\,20,\\,24,\\,28,\\,32,…$\n\n- Multiples of $7:$ $\\,7,\\,14,\\,21,\\,28,\\,35,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(4,7)=28.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(z+2)^{2}(w-5)^{2}$ and $(z+2)^{3}(w-5)^{4},$ we find the largest powers for $(z+2)$ and $(w-5)$ that appear in the expressions:\n\n- For $(z+2)^{2}$ and $(z+2)^{3},$ the largest power is $3.$\n\n- For $(w-5)^{2}$ and $(w-5)^{4},$ the largest power is $4.$\n\nNow, we raise $(z+2)$ and $(w-5)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((z+2)^{2}(w-5)^{2},\\,(z+2)^{3}(w-5)^{4})=(z+2)^{3}\\cdot (w-5)^{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(4(z+2)^{2}(w-5)^{2},\\,7(z+2)^{3}(w-5)^{4})=28(z+2)^{3}(w-5)^{4}.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246755 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247238 1069 "q-111807" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247238 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247238 1071 "The matrix $R$ is given below. Let ${r}_{1}$, ${r}_{2}$,  ${r}_{3}$ and ${r}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $R.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(R).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${r}_{4}={x}_{1}{r}_{1}+{x}_{2}{r}_{2}+{x}_{3}{r}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -10 & -10\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247238 1072 #uuid "4d5f352a-5b0e-55b2-a382-bea7929379c7" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247238 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $R$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -10 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -10 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(R)$ is ${{r}_{1},{r}_{2}}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B={{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(R).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247239 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246640 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247239 1069 "q-111809" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247239 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247239 1071 "The matrix $S$ is given below. Let ${s}_{1}$, ${s}_{2}$,  ${s}_{3}$ and ${s}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $S.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{s}_{1},{s}_{2},{s}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(S).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${s}_{4}={x}_{1}{s}_{1}+{x}_{2}{s}_{2}+{x}_{3}{s}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nS=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247239 1072 #uuid "7fa86926-dc53-59ca-bada-4e3c0c5873bf" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247239 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $S$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(S)$ is ${{s}_{1},{s}_{2}}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B={{s}_{1},{s}_{2},{s}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(S).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246558 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247240 1069 "q-11183" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247240 1071 "![](images/96/96a2d22126a8f2575ae97bae8e4a20ef6e7a99fc888b2ce7102c731963d63fea.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve $x={y}^{2}+2y,$ the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=-2,$ as shown above." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247240 1072 #uuid "ad7fb34b-ef8f-5260-8235-4ecc480cd213" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247240 1074 "To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}({y}^{2}+2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{-2}^{0}+2\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =(0-\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3})+2(0-\\frac{(-2)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}-4 \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $|-\\frac{4}{3}|=\\frac{4}{3}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246695 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247241 1069 "q-11186" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247241 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247241 1071 "![](images/18/1840ce2886aa3546371998239757a5dc500449007dc511dc0860b1ce59a59d2c.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve $x=\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}+2y,$ the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=2,$ as shown above." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247241 1072 #uuid "709ff7ee-033f-586e-bef4-cc085767454b" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247241 1074 "To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}+2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+2{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}+2\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{8}{3}-0)+2(2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{44}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246714 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247242 1069 "q-11193" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247242 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247242 1071 "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-3)^{n+1}}{{10}^{n}}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247242 1072 #uuid "1c5912c6-ae04-5053-9bf1-3ad4258de6f2" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247242 1074 "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{(-3)^{n+1}}{{10}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\cdot {(-3)}^{n}}{{10}^{n}} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{{(-3)}^{n}}{{10}^{n}} \\\\  & =-3{(-\\frac{3}{10})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now see that this is the geometric sequence with $r=-\\frac{3}{10}.$ \n\nSince $|r|<1,$ the sequence converges to $0.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246733 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247243 1069 "q-112047" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247243 1071 "Factor the expression ${g}^{4}-1\\,296{h}^{4}.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that $1\\,296={36}^{2}.$*" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247243 1072 #uuid "da65b5a9-9f7e-5e08-81cc-28c86234e09b" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247243 1074 "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{g}^{4}-1\\,296{h}^{4}={({g}^{2})}^{2}-{(36{h}^{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{({g}^{2})}^{2}-{(36{h}^{2})}^{2}=({g}^{2}+36{h}^{2})({g}^{2}-36{h}^{2})\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{g}^{2}-36{h}^{2}={g}^{2}-(6h)^{2}\n$$\n\n Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({g}^{2}+36{h}^{2})({g}^{2}-36{h}^{2}) & =({g}^{2}+36{h}^{2})(g+6h)(g-6h)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246798 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247244 1069 "q-112063" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247244 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247244 1071 "Consider an ellipse such that its major axis $a$ can vary while its minor axis is fixed. What is the best interpretation of ${S}^{′}(a)$ if $S$ is the area enclosed by the ellipse?" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247244 1072 #uuid "45b2e799-d000-5801-973f-7fb80701d236" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247244 1074 "The function $S(a)$ gives the area of the ellipse with major axis $a.$ So,\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(a)=\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}a}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }S}{\\text{Change in }a}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${S}^{′}(a)$ is the rate at which the area changes as the major axis changes." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246727 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247245 1069 "q-112076" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247245 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247245 1071 "![](images/83/83b8d9a8d8f1cd9cc79fb10964e0d34c38204cc518a1e5175ac9a826127cd548.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y={x}^{3}-4x?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247245 1072 #uuid "521eada5-fcae-5914-a56b-54aece215449" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247245 1074 "Let's call $P(x)$ the given polynomial:\n\n$$\nP(x)={x}^{3}-4x\n$$\n\nThe degree $(n=3)$ is odd and the leading coefficient $({a}_{3}=1)$ is positive. Therefore, the polynomial has the following end behavior:\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nOnly the graph $A$ satisfies these conditions." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246706 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247246 1069 "q-112091" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247246 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247246 1071 "The function $S(t)$ gives the area, in squared centimeters, of a thin sheet of ice as it melts $t$ minutes after observations start. What is the best interpretation of ${S}^{′}(5)=-7?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247246 1072 #uuid "152b8d9d-0ba8-513e-8b4b-b5c65402cd59" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247246 1074 "The function ${S}^{′}(t)$ represents the rate at which the area of the sheet is decreasing $t$ minutes after the beginning of the observation.\n\nTherefore, since it is negative, ${S}^{′}(5)=-7$ means:\n\n$\\,$ *$5$ minutes after the observations began, the area of the sheet decreases at a rate of $7$ squared centimeters per minute.*" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246752 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247247 1069 "q-112096" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247247 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247247 1071 "![](images/b8/b8bbe0874d3fab822db551541916b6792d79399f41ac73f4ce119c9aebb6bc68.png)\n\nWhich of the following polynomials could be the function shown in the diagram above?" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247247 1072 #uuid "b04e5341-6d56-50ea-b140-037ef05c555e" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247247 1074 "Let's call $P(x)$ the polynomial shown in the diagram.\n\nThe end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is odd, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is positive.\n\n- the $y$-intercept is $-1,$ which means that $P(0)=-1.$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the only one that satisfies all three criteria is $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-1.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246883 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247248 1069 "q-112098" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247248 1071 "![](images/aa/aad161b50958b85f30421bf7420faf87ff4dc291ca1bb6d233626d67a42885ba.png)\n\nWhich of the following polynomials could be the function shown in the diagram above?" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247248 1072 #uuid "02127b95-c738-5c7a-b156-b78126fa0399" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247248 1074 "Let's call $P(x)$ the polynomial shown in the diagram.\n\nThe end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is odd, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is positive.\n\n- the $y$-intercept is $3,$ which means that $P(0)=3.$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the only one that satisfies all three criteria is $y=2{x}^{5}-6{x}^{2}+3.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186247001 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247249 1069 "q-112114" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247249 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247249 1071 "![](images/b4/b46093feaf5d7138e30c6d6fc673a2d915cac74a23ae76e0841e9bb5b135fa60.png)\n\nThe graph of the polynomial $y=P(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The degree of $P(x)$ is even.\n- The coefficient of the leading term is positive.\n- The constant term is positive." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247249 1072 #uuid "57caf7d5-ab2c-51c1-a72f-c7af8b4259a1" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247249 1074 "The end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is even, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative.\n\nSo, statement I is true, while statement II is false.\n\nLastly, note that the $y$-intercept is positive, and therefore the constant term must be positive. So, III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247250 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246914 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247250 1069 "q-112115" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247250 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247250 1071 "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247250 1072 #uuid "24c16e18-9367-5d2d-9490-8777d313d56b" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247250 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=-2{x}_{3},$ where ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247251 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246822 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247251 1069 "q-112149" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247251 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247251 1071 "Given the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]$, find a basis of $\\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247251 1072 #uuid "6a443b9d-71e4-51fa-a6eb-49cd9e6d62f7" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247251 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 9 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, equation $Ax=0$ is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+9{x}_{3}-4{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}+3{x}_{4}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is ${x}_{1}=-9{x}_{3}+4{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{2}=6{x}_{3}-3{x}_{4},$ where ${x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are free variables. Therefore, if $x\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-9{x}_{3}+4{x}_{4} \\\\ 6{x}_{3}-3{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9{x}_{3} \\\\ 6{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{4} \\\\ -3{x}_{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3},{x}_{4}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\text{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246830 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247252 1069 "q-11216" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247252 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247252 1071 "![](images/b4/b4408c4dc92d7145fef94603269f2cc54ceeb3bb38703401ff46004a2428dd3f.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve $x=-{y}^{5}+1,$ the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1,$ as shown above." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247252 1072 #uuid "2742531e-a0c1-549c-b37f-4b5ab715a95d" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247252 1074 "To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-{y}^{5}+1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\frac{{y}^{6}}{6}|}_{0}^{1}+y{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{6}-0)+(1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246601 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247253 1069 "q-112197" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247253 1071 "Find the vector $x$, if $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ and \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247253 1072 #uuid "e4a567cc-00f9-5ef3-a682-d76360618a8f" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247253 1074 "We know that any vector in  $\\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,B.$ Since we are told that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={{(\\,6\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+{{(\\,-7\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -30 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ 21 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246630 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247254 1069 "q-112203" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247254 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247254 1072 #uuid "df51ea15-f830-55ce-a2e9-e42267e5f4de" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247254 1074 "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,8\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=3\\cdot {b}_{1}+8\\cdot {b}_{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that ${x}_{1}=3.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247255 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246651 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247255 1069 "q-112204" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247255 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247255 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}},$ where \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247255 1072 #uuid "1636206a-9151-5198-a421-3985f6d14532" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247255 1074 "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,4\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,0\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}+{{(\\,5\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{3}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=4\\cdot {b}_{1}+0\\cdot {b}_{2}+5\\cdot {b}_{3}\n$$\n\nand therefore \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}=4.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247256 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246747 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247256 1069 "q-112208" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247256 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247256 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{2}?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247256 1072 #uuid "438d91ee-b10a-5c27-8e0b-9fc621fd3a05" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247256 1074 "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,2\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}+{{(\\,6\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{3}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=2\\cdot {b}_{1}+3\\cdot {b}_{2}+6\\cdot {b}_{3}\n$$\n\nand therefore \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{2}=3.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247257 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246589 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247257 1069 "q-112230" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247257 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247257 1071 "Find the least common multiple of the polynomials $5(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3},$ $\\,6(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{3},$ and $12(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{2}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247257 1072 #uuid "467c944d-a18c-50f2-9456-d9d3072e545c" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247257 1074 "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of three polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(5,6,12):$\n\n- Multiples of $5:$ $\\,5,\\,10,…,\\,55,\\,60,\\,65,\\,…$\n\n- Multiples of $6:$ $\\,6,\\,12,…,\\,54,\\,60,\\,66,…$\n\n- Multiples of $12:$ $\\,12,\\,24,\\,36,\\,48,\\,60,\\,72,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(5,6,12)=60.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3},(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{3},$ and $(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{2},$ we find the largest powers for $(x-5)$ and $(y-2)$ that appear in all these expressions:\n\n- For $(x-5)^{5},(x-5)^{3},$ and $(x-5)^{3},$ the largest power is $5.$\n\n- For $(y-2)^{3},(y-2)^{3},$ and $(y-2)^{2},$ the largest power is $3.$\n\nNow, we raise $(x-5)$ and $(y-2)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3},\\,(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{3},\\,(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{2})=(x-5)^{5}\\cdot (y-2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{LCM}(5(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3},\\,6(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{3},\\,12(x-5)^{3}(y-2)^{2}) & = \\\\ 60\\cdot (x-5)^{5}\\cdot (y-2)^{3} & = \\\\ 60(x-5)^{5}(y-2)^{3} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246915 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247258 1069 "q-112248" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247258 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247258 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 41\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247258 1072 #uuid "63a14e88-cc23-5be1-a9f6-6129753bc256" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247258 1074 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 41\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 41\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 41\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -13 & 26\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ -13{x}_{2}=26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3(-2)=-3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=(-9)+(-2)=-11.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246895 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247259 1069 "q-112249" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247259 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247259 1071 "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247259 1072 #uuid "8563367f-0e91-5d7d-a84d-c182442533d2" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247259 1074 "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -25 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247260 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246838 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247260 1069 "q-112252" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247260 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247260 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247260 1072 #uuid "eeced2e5-384e-5308-b21f-d88eb6c697e3" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247260 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-4I:$\n\n$$\nA-4I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-4I:$\n\n$$\n(A-4I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-4I:$\n\n$$\n(A-4I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""