e Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(2).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(2) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $z$ is an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-2I)z=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247283 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246950 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247283 1069 "q-112488" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247283 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247283 1071 "The equation ${n}^{2}-11=2n$ can be written in the form $(n+p{)}^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247283 1072 #uuid "db749aa2-b405-5990-8407-5b43744a1377" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247283 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, the $2n$ term to the left-hand side, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-11 & =2n \\\\ {n}^{2}-2n & =11 \\\\ {n}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot n & =11 \\\\ {n}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot n+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =11 \\\\ (n-1{)}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n-1{)}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =11 \\\\ (n-1{)}^{2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=-1,q=12,$ and $p+q=11.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n-1{)}^{2} & =12 \\\\ \\sqrt{(n-1{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\ |n-1| & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\ n-1 & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ n & =1\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247284 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246917 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247284 1069 "q-112490" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247284 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247284 1071 "The equation ${a}^{2}-12a+18=0$ can be written in the form $(a-p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247284 1072 #uuid "ccd1081d-908d-5372-a315-9be341f0eb2c" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247284 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}-12a+18 & =0 \\\\ {a}^{2}-12a & =-18 \\\\ {a}^{2}-2\\cdot 6\\cdot a+{6}^{2}-{6}^{2} & =-18 \\\\ (a-6)^{2}-{6}^{2} & =-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a-6)^{2}-{6}^{2} & =-18 \\\\ (a-6)^{2}-36 & =-18 \\\\ (a-6)^{2} & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=6,q=18,$ and $p+q=24.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a-6)^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\sqrt{(a-6)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\ |a-6| & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\ a-6 & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ a & =6\\pm 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246969 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247285 1069 "q-112497" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247285 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247285 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a