71 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-16 & -25 \\\\ 9 & 14\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247286 1072 #uuid "7cebb59d-20db-57ac-95ff-8484c20edb72" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247286 1074 "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(-1).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-(-1)I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-(-1)I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =-1.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 & -25 \\\\ 9 & 14\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-15 & -25 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $z$ is an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-(-1)I)z=[\\begin{aligned}-15 & -25 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-(-1)I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246737 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247287 1069 "q-112506" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247287 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247287 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247287 1072 #uuid "00d0e6df-22e0-54bb-9650-150f7f0d8e32" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247287 1074 "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(1).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =1.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $y$ is an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-1I)y=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246762 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247288 1069 "q-112507" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247288 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247288 1071 "The rate $O$ of the orders a car factory receives can be modeled as $O(t)=100t{e}^{-0.1t}$ cars per day. The rate at which cars are completed can be modeled as $C(t)=50+10sin⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{7}t)$ cars per day. In both cases, $t$ is the time, in days, since the launch of the new model. \n\nWhat is the rate of change in the number of cars under construction $28$ days after the model's launch? Assume that an ordered car goes into construction as soon as the factory receives its order. Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247288 1072 #uuid "b547e5e1-1e78-5d71-9ff5-a1728add6182" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247288 1074 "The rate of change of the number of cars under construction at time $t$ is equal to the order rate minus the completion rate, that is,\n\n$$\nO(t)-C(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=28,$ the rate of change in the number of cars under construction after $28$ days is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}O(t)-C(t) & =100(28){e}^{-0.1(28)}-(50+10sin⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{7}(28))) \\\\  & =100(28){e}^{-2.8}-50-10sin⁡(8\\pi ) \\\\  & =100(28){e}^{-2.8}-50 \\\\  & ≈120.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change in the number of cars in construction after $28$ days is $120$ cars per day." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246624 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247289 1069 "q-112518" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247289 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247289 1071 "Liam, a farmer, is monitoring the construction of a fence by recording the following two numbers for different values of $t,$ where $t$ is the time, in hours, since he started his observations:\n\n- $P(t)$ - the number of panels in the fence.\n\n- $r(t)$ - the rate at which the number of panels in the fence increases.\n\nWhat is the relationship between $P(t)$ and $r(t)?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247289 1072 #uuid "59ff5d13-6b87-5fe2-a8ce-d480b49dc41c" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247289 1074 "The function $P(t)$ gives the number of panels in the fence after $t$ hours. Then,\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}P(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }P}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${P}^{′}(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of panels increases at time $t$, which is the same as $r(t).$ Therefore, the relationship between $P(t)$ and $r(t)$ is ${P}^{′}(t)=r(t).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247290 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246613 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247290 1069 "q-112519" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247290 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247290 1071 "To measure the number of children visiting a playground, Noah uses the following three functions that model the hourly number of visitors to the playground $t$ hours after the playground is opened to the public.\n\n- $N(t)$ - the number of children inside the playground\n\n- $E(t)$ - the rate at which children enter the playground\n\n- $L(t)$ - the rate at which children leave the playground\n\nWhat is the relationship between the functions above?" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247290 1072 #uuid "e51a9487-572c-5eee-b066-402d8c949221" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247290 1074 "The function $N(t)$ gives the number of children inside the park $t$ minutes after opening. Then,\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}N(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }N}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${N}^{′}(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of children inside the playground changes $t$ hours after opening.\n\nIn addition, we can compute the rate of change of the number of children inside the playground at time $t$ by finding the difference between the entry and exit rates at time $t.$ That is,\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(t)=E(t)-L(t).\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246704 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247291 1069 "q-112522" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247291 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247291 1071 "Oliver, the owner of a platform for chess players, uses the following three functions to model the daily number of users of the platform $t$ days after it was launched.\n\n- $N(t)$ - the number of users with an account\n\n- $O(t)$ - the rate at which new users open an account\n\n- $C(t)$ - the rate at which users cancel their account\n\nWhat is the relationship between the three functions above?" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247291 1072 #uuid "a8187edc-1d98-57d1-8d80-0dd8b47814cc" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247291 1074 "The function $N(t)$ gives the number of users $t$ days after the platform is set up. Then,\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}N(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }N}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${N}^{′}(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of users with an account changes $t$ days after opening.\n\nIn addition, we can compute the rate of change of the number of users at time $t$ by finding the difference between the open and cancel rates at time $t.$ That is,\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(t)=O(t)-C(t).\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246968 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247292 1069 "q-112561" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247292 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247292 1071 "Let $(x,y)$ be the solution of the following system. What is the value of $x+y?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=7 \\\\ 0.5x+0.4y=0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247292 1072 #uuid "67d9ca65-c314-5c48-b66e-c63d3eedec32" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247292 1074 "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the second equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=7 &  \\\\ 0.5x+0.4y=0.2 & |\\,\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=7 \\\\ 5x+4y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the  first equation by $-5,$ then we will get a $-5x$ term that can cancel with the $5x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=7 & |\\,\\times (-5) \\\\ 5x+4y=2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-5x-15y=-35 \\\\ 5x+4y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable and allows us to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}-\\cancel{5x}-15y & =-35 \\\\ \\cancel{5x}+4y & =2 \\\\ (-15y+4y) & =(-33) \\\\ -11y & =-33 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $y=3$ into the equation $x+3y=7$ to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x+3y & =7 \\\\ x+3(3) & =7 \\\\ x+9 & =7 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-2,\\,y=3.$ So,\n\n$$\nx+y=(-2)+(3)=1.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246855 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247293 1069 "q-112562" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247293 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247293 1071 "Let $(x,y)$ be the solution of the following system. What is the value of $x+y?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x+3y=15 \\\\ 0.6x-0.8y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247293 1072 #uuid "e808096a-9ed1-5b7c-b4a9-06ec77b95419" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247293 1074 "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the second equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x+3y=15 &  \\\\ 0.6x-0.8y=1 & |\\,\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}4x+3y=15 \\\\ 6x-8y=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $3$ and the second equation by $-2,$ we will get a $12x$ term in the first equation that can cancel with the $-12x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x+3y=15 & |\\,\\times 3 \\\\ 6x-8y=10 & |\\,\\times -2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}12x+9y=45 \\\\ -12x+16y=-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable and allows us to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{12x}+9y & =45 \\\\ -\\cancel{12x}+16y & =-20 \\\\ (9y+16y) & =(45-20) \\\\ 25y & =25 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $y=1$ into the equation $4x+3y=15$ to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3y & =15 \\\\ 4x+3 & =15 \\\\ 4x & =15-3 \\\\ 4x & =12 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=3$ and $y=1.$ So, \n\n$$\nx+y=3+1=4.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247294 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246663 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247294 1069 "q-112564" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247294 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247294 1071 "Let $(a,b)$ be the solution of the following system. What is the value of $a+b?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}a+1.5b-6=0 \\\\ 0.6a-0.4b-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247294 1072 #uuid "6c89904c-c1f7-52b5-8b35-359906e3c495" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247294 1074 "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply both equations by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}a+1.5b-6=0 & |\\,\\times 10 \\\\ 0.6a-0.4b-1=0 & |\\,\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}10a+15b-60=0 \\\\ 6a-4b-10=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we  multiply the first equation by $\\frac{2}{5}$ and the second equation by $\\frac{3}{2},$ we will get a $6b$ term in the first equation that can cancel with the $-6b$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}10a+15b-60=0 & |\\,\\times \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 6a-4b-10=0 & |\\,\\times \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}4a+6b-24=0 \\\\ 9a-6b-15=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $b$-variable and allows us to solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a+\\cancel{6b}-24 & =0 \\\\ 9a-\\cancel{6b}-15 & =0 \\\\ (4a+9a)+(-24-15) & =0 \\\\ 13a-39 & =0 \\\\ a & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $a=3$ into the equation $6a-4b-10=0$ to solve for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a-4b-10 & =0 \\\\ 6(3)-4b-10 & =0 \\\\ 18-4b-10 & =0 \\\\ 8-4b & =0 \\\\ b & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $a=3$ and $b=2.$ So, \n\n$$\na+b=3+2=5.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246790 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247295 1069 "q-112585" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247295 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247295 1071 "![](images/5e/5e4e2241490626139e15800f3b90c8e15a9963bdcace9907f8557b0897d533e6.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $p(0)$ is positive.\n- $p(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n- $x=0$ is a root of odd multiplicity." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247295 1072 #uuid "71e1e299-84ff-5a79-94c6-c0078815dc95" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247295 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The graph passes through the $y$-axis at $y=0,$ which means that $p(0)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ moves right in the positive direction towards $\\infty ,$ the $y$ value moves down in the negative direction towards $-\\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. The root $x=0$ has an odd multiplicity because the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247296 1068 #edb/ref 17592186247000 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247296 1069 "q-112587" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247296 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247296 1071 "![](images/aa/aa431232a1f9ef2374f4e8a9c0202cf60ce1dcfef69c2a6fca604fe6aed7b681.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $p(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n- $p(0)=0.$\n- $x=1$ is a root of even multiplicity." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247296 1072 #uuid "576d8616-a0cd-59d9-a961-712779c4c308" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247296 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ moves left in the negative direction towards $-\\infty ,$ the $y$ value moves down in the negative direction towards $-\\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. The graph passes through the $y$-axis at $y=0$, which means that $p(0)=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The root $x=1$ has an odd multiplicity because the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247297 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246826 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247297 1069 "q-112589" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247297 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247297 1071 "The equation $6{z}^{2}+10z-1=0$ can be written in the form $6(z+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247297 1072 #uuid "165c4c89-9a6c-555e-8270-074787fb6a50" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247297 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{z}^{2}+10z-1 & =0 \\\\ 6{z}^{2}+10z & =1 \\\\ 6({z}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}z) & =1 \\\\ 6[{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot z+{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}] & =1 \\\\ 6[{(z+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}] & =1 \\\\ 6[{(z+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{36}] & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{(z+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-6\\cdot \\frac{25}{36} & =1 \\\\ 6{(z+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{6} & =1 \\\\ 6{(z+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2} & =\\frac{31}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{5}{6}$ and $q=\\frac{31}{6}.$ Finally,\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{5}{6}+\\frac{31}{6}=\\frac{36}{6}=6.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247298 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246600 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247298 1069 "q-112595" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247298 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247298 1071 "![](images/a5/a5f078132f264a73a4cf2cd1c6f43c61e7f396fa0d6fc8daf2f979c3665ed83e.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247298 1072 #uuid "f607d0ef-b074-533f-961b-0d500f86758f" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247298 1074 "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-3,$ $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-3,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=0,$ this root has even multiplicity.\n\n- Since the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=2,$ this root has even multiplicity.\n\nTherefore, the only possible equation is $y=-\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{2}(x+3)(x-2{)}^{4}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247299 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246872 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247299 1069 "q-112596" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247299 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247299 1071 "![](images/84/849535b983a64c97b4cf06afcc7da7598349dc3f14f771cd3ae20db2c0742e8b.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247299 1072 #uuid "ac9b5d16-e129-5bbc-9ccb-344cad0daab4" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247299 1074 "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-2,$ $x=0$ and $x=3.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-2,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=0,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=0,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=3,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\nTherefore, the only possible equation is $y=\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{3}(x+2)(x-3).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246879 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247300 1069 "q-112599" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247300 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247300 1071 "![](images/15/1586e286d2b07aac28a42f91c76318ae209c46c19e14e429c62f0770ada9393b.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=-x(x+2)^{2}(x-2)^{3}?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247300 1072 #uuid "e82163b8-d12f-5c46-89d0-bd29c13ba90d" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247300 1074 "Based on the factored equation, we can make the following conclusions about the graph:\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=-2$ is $2,$ which is even, the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at this point.\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=0$ is $1,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at this point. Moreover, because $x=0$ is a simple root, the graph must *not* be tangent to the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=2$ is $3,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at this point. Moreover, because $x=2$ is a triple-root, the graph must be tangent to the $x$-axis at this point.\n\nOnly the graphs $\\text{I}$ and $\\text{IV}$ satisfy these conditions.\n\nTo choose between these two graphs, we need to consider the end behavior. Since the leading coefficient is negative, as $x$ approaches $\\infty ,$ $y$ approaches $-\\infty .$ Since only graph $\\text{I}$ displays this end behavior, the correct answer is $\\text{I}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246840 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247301 1069 "q-112605" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247301 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247301 1071 "The equation $-6{t}^{2}+5t-1=0$ can be written in the form $6(t-p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247301 1072 #uuid "bdfe00ea-c67d-5b81-860c-24f0330009dc" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247301 1074 "First, we multiply the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-6{t}^{2}+5t-1) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}-5t+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}-5t+1 & =0 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}-5t & =-1 \\\\ 6({t}^{2}-\\frac{5}{6}t) & =-1 \\\\ 6[{t}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{12}t+{(\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}] & =-1 \\\\ 6[{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{144}] & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}-6\\cdot \\frac{25}{144} & =-1 \\\\ 6{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{24} & =-1 \\\\ 6{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2} & =-1+\\frac{25}{24} \\\\ 6{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{5}{12}.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{24} \\\\ {(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{144} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(t-\\frac{5}{12})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{144}} \\\\ |t-\\frac{5}{12}| & =\\frac{1}{12} \\\\ t-\\frac{5}{12} & =\\pm \\frac{1}{12} \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{12}\\pm \\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{5}{12}-\\frac{1}{12}=\\frac{4}{12}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{12}+\\frac{1}{12}=\\frac{6}{12}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $t=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246769 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247302 1069 "q-112607" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247302 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247302 1071 "The equation $-4{x}^{2}-32x-61=0$ can be written in the form $4(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247302 1072 #uuid "5d5a2dc1-b091-5d17-b744-bab152c4767b" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247302 1074 "First, we multiply the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{x}^{2}-32x-61) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+32x+61 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+32x+61 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+32x & =-61 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+8x) & =-61 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4\\cdot x+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2}] & =-61 \\\\ 4[{(x+4)}^{2}-16] & =-61\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+4)}^{2}-4\\cdot 16 & =-61 \\\\ 4{(x+4)}^{2}-64 & =-61 \\\\ 4{(x+4)}^{2} & =-61+64 \\\\ 4{(x+4)}^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=3.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+4)}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {(x+4)}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x+4)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\ |x+4| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x & =-4\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246828 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247303 1069 "q-112615" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247303 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247303 1071 "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(x+1)P(x).\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247303 1072 #uuid "904dff05-ecb6-51b3-917d-26bcf8537c87" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247303 1074 "Let $f(x)=3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6.$ We know that $(x-3)$ and $(x+1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $3$ | $-13$ | $7$ | $17$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $9$ | $-12$ | $-15$ | $6$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-4$ | $-5$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6}{x-3}=3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $-5$ | $2$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-3$ | $7$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-7$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x+2}{x+1}=3{x}^{2}-7x+2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(x+1)(3{x}^{2}-7x+2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-13{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+17x-6=(x-3)(x+1){{(3x-1)(x-2)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(3x-1)(x-2).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246590 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247304 1069 "q-112617" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247304 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247304 1071 "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24=(x-3)(x+4)P(x).\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247304 1072 #uuid "d4055097-e27e-5747-8711-3ff850d11a98" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247304 1074 "Let $f(x)=3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24.$ We know that $(x-3)$ and $(x+4)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $3$ | $4$ | $-37$ | $-14$ | $24$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $9$ | $39$ | $6$ | $-24$ |\n|  | $3$ | $13$ | $2$ | $-8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24}{x-3}=3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24=(x-3)(3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+4)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+4)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $3$ | $13$ | $2$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-12$ | $-4$ | $8$ |\n|  | $3$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+2x-8}{x+4}=3{x}^{2}+x-2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24=(x-3)(x+4)(3{x}^{2}+x-2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-37{x}^{2}-14x+24=(x-3)(x+4){{(3x-2)(x+1)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(3x-2)(x+1).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247305 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246771 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247305 1069 "q-112625" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247305 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247305 1071 "The equation $-4{w}^{2}-16w+9=0$ can be written in the form $4(w+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247305 1072 #uuid "7a14e1ad-9f79-57f9-93d0-d6d12002fa7d" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247305 1074 "First, we multiply the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{w}^{2}-16w+9) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}+16w-9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{2}+16w-9 & =0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}+16w & =9 \\\\ 4({w}^{2}+4w) & =9 \\\\ 4[{w}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot w+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}] & =9 \\\\ 4[{(w+2)}^{2}-4] & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w+2)}^{2}-4\\cdot 4 & =9 \\\\ 4{(w+2)}^{2}-16 & =9 \\\\ 4{(w+2)}^{2} & =9+16 \\\\ 4{(w+2)}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=2.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w+2)}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {(w+2)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(w+2)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |w+2| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w+2 & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w & =-2\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =-2-\\frac{5}{2}=-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ w & =-2+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $w=-\\frac{9}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246635 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247306 1069 "q-112626" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247306 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247306 1071 "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)(x+1)P(x).\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247306 1072 #uuid "7c8eab9f-7b10-5db7-a80e-51f13f149526" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247306 1074 "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35.$ We know that $(x-5)$ and $(x+1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-5)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-5)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-5)$ using synthetic division:\n\n\\\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-36$ | $-2$ | $35$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $5$ | $35$ | $-5$ | $-35$ |\n|  | $1$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $-7$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35}{x-5}={x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)({x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $-7$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $7$ |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $-7$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7}{x+1}={x}^{2}+6x-7,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)(x+1)({x}^{2}+6x-7).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-36{x}^{2}-2x+35=(x-5)(x+1){{(x-1)(x+7)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=(x-1)(x+7).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246605 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247307 1069 "q-112632" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247307 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247307 1071 "Let $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ where ${b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}$ and ${b}_{2}=-{c}_{1}?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247307 1072 #uuid "1a40e36f-1c5f-56d9-8cff-9a214e68eceb" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247307 1074 "Since $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\  & ={c}_{2}+2{c}_{1} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2})-2(-{c}_{1}) \\\\  & =2{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247308 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246594 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247308 1069 "q-112638" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247308 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247308 1071 "Let $p(x)={x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-35{x}^{2}-57x+90.$ Given that $p(-3)=p(1)=0,$ find the sum of all distinct real roots of $p(x).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247308 1072 #uuid "2583d164-46b3-52de-80d0-28acb646d7d5" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247308 1074 "Since $p(-3)=p(1)=0,$ by factor theorem $(x+3)$ and $(x-1)$ are both factors of $p(x).$\nTo factor $p(x)$ completely we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x-1)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-35$ | $-57$ | $90$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-33$ | $-90$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $-33$ | $-90$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-35{x}^{2}-57x+90}{x-1}={x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-33x-90,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-35{x}^{2}-57x+90=(x-1)({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-33x-90).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+3)$ is also a factor of $p(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-33x-90).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-33$ | $-90$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-3$ | $3$ | $90$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-1$ | $-30$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-33x-90}{x+3}={x}^{2}-x-30,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-35{x}^{2}-57x+90=(x-1)(x+3)({x}^{2}-x-30).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-35{x}^{2}-57x+90=(x-1)(x+3)(x-6)(x+5).\n$$\n\nWe can now equate each factor to zero and solve for $x$ to find the three roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1\\,,\\,x=-3\\,,\\,x=6\\,,\\,x=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the roots of the polynomial is\n\n$$\n1-3+6-5=-1.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246682 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247309 1069 "q-112639" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247309 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247309 1071 "Let $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ where ${b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}$ and ${b}_{2}=2{c}_{1}?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247309 1072 #uuid "4f429b4c-0ce4-5b13-a3b9-ab9f0ad3c41b" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247309 1074 "Since $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={c}_{1}+3{c}_{2} \\\\  & =3{c}_{2}+{c}_{1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2})+\\frac{1}{2}(2{c}_{1}) \\\\  & =9{b}_{1}+\\frac