}^{2}+9$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac={0}^{2}-4(1)(9)=-36<0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}+9$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative. \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-10{x}^{3}+34{x}^{2}-90x+225=(x-5)^{2}{{({x}^{2}+9)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $P(x)={x}^{2}+9.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246794 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247311 1069 "q-112652" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247311 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247311 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}},$ where ${c}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}{b}_{3}$, ${c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{3}=\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{1}?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247311 1072 #uuid "af6dc83a-8496-5a89-b076-ffb3a3527cae" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247311 1074 "Since $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}-{b}_{3} \\\\  & =-{b}_{3}+2{b}_{2}+5{b}_{1} \\\\  & =5(-\\frac{1}{5}{b}_{3})+2\\cdot 2(\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{2})+5\\cdot 2(\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =5{c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}+10{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246561 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247312 1069 "q-112656" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247312 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247312 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}},$ where ${c}_{1}=4{b}_{3}$, ${c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}$ and ${c}_{3}=\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{2}?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247312 1072 #uuid "f5c48c40-2fd0-5515-867f-1f3be83f3dfb" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247312 1074 "Since $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}+8{b}_{3} \\\\  & =8{b}_{3}+2{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =2(4{b}_{3})+2{b}_{1}+2\\cdot 2(\\frac{1}{2}{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+4{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246611 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247313 1069 "q-112810" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247313 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247313 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}},$ where ${c}_{1}=-{b}_{2}$, ${c}_{2}=2{b}_{3}$ and ${c}_{3}=-2{b}_{1}?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247313 1072 #uuid "cbd24e92-59cc-5dbc-90fb-0d504396afb4" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247313 1074 "Since $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{b}_{1}-4{b}_{2}+2{b}_{3} \\\\  & =-4{b}_{2}+2{b}_{3}+2{b}_{1} \\\\  & =4(-{b}_{2})+2{b}_{3}-(-2{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}-{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247314 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246900 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247314 1069 "q-112833" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247314 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247314 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}=-4{b}_{1}+5{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247314 1072 #uuid "118ecf4b-5c6a-550a-b2fc-aa5d498bd2a9" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247314 1074 "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=-4{b}_{1}+5{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. Multiplying the second equation by $5$ and adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+5{c}_{2}=6{b}_{1}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{1}+\\frac{5}{6}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=-4(\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{1}+\\frac{5}{6}{c}_{2})+5{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{2}{6}{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{6}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3{b}_{1}-6{b}_{2} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{1}+\\frac{5}{6}{c}_{2})-6(\\frac{2}{6}{c}_{1}+\\frac{4}{6}{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{c}_{1}-\\frac{3}{2}{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247315 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246963 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247315 1069 "q-112855" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247315 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247315 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}=2{b}_{1}+3{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}={b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247315 1072 #uuid "9c8b3d18-f99b-57ac-806c-9402fb0d526b" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247315 1074 "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=2{b}_{1}+3{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}={b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. Multiplying the second equation by $-2$ and adding the equations, we get  \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}=-{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=-{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{2}={b}_{1}+2(-{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=2{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2} \\\\  & =2(2{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2})-3(-{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}-6{c}_{2}+3{c}_{1}-6{c}_{2} \\\\  & =7{c}_{1}-12{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247316 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246988 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247316 1069 "q-112865" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247316 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247316 1071 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+x-6?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247316 1072 #uuid "f373b73a-46eb-5368-a73d-06fed58756d5" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247316 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-6,$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-6}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{2}{3}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(2)$ is a factor of $-6$ and the denominator $(3)$ is a factor of $3.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246848 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247317 1069 "q-112873" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247317 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247317 1071 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{3}-4x+5?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247317 1072 #uuid "44aca3eb-a3e1-543f-a007-6610ed8f8b3e" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247317 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $5$ and the leading coefficient is $8.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }5}{\\text{factor of }8}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{5}{6}$ does not take the above form. The denominator $(6)$ is not a factor of $8.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247318 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246579 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247318 1069 "q-112874" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247318 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247318 1071 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=10{x}^{5}-7{x}^{2}+2x-8?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247318 1072 #uuid "9ec8a80b-994e-55b9-8685-24fefa177863" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247318 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-8$ and the leading coefficient is $10.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-8}{\\text{factor of }10}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-\\frac{6}{5}=\\frac{-6}{5}$ does not take the above form. The numerator $(-6)$ is not a factor of $-8.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247319 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246652 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247319 1069 "q-112875" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247319 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247319 1071 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-4?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247319 1072 #uuid "b6b933a9-c0e8-5275-ae25-ac03d96707b1" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247319 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-4$ and the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-4}{\\text{factor of }2}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-3$ does not take the above form. The value $-3$ can be interpreted as a fraction $-\\frac{3}{1},$ and the numerator $(3)$ is not a factor of $-4.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247320 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246989 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247320 1069 "q-112876" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247320 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247320 1071 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=14{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}-x-7?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247320 1072 #uuid "aa7f9c70-0361-57e7-9796-46db5368f8ef" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247320 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-7$ and the leading coefficient is $14.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-7}{\\text{factor of }14}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{1}{21}$ does not take the above form. The denominator $(21)$ is not a factor of $14.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247321 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246913 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247321 1069 "q-112877" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247321 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247321 1071 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=-6{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-9x+9?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247321 1072 #uuid "20875202-2f78-5deb-8279-48996bbf9d5a" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247321 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $9$ and the leading coefficient is $-6.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }9}{\\text{factor of }-6}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-2$ does not take the above form. The value $-2$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{2}{-1},$ and the numerator $(2)$ is not a factor of $9.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247322 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246928 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247322 1069 "q-11290" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247322 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247322 1071 "$2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247322 1072 #uuid "abf2a161-03cb-55e0-99c1-1308ac08b447" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247322 1074 "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\ ϕ & ≈0.983\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡(ϕ)+{R}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡(ϕ) & ={3}^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡(ϕ)+{cos}^{2}⁡(ϕ)) & =9+4 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =13 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)≈\\sqrt{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.983)\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247323 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246795 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247323 1069 "q-11291" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247323 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247323 1071 "$4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247323 1072 #uuid "ba7d09f7-efa8-5411-b860-5a937a2a4958" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247323 1074 "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & ≈0.896\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{R}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ & ={5}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =25+16 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =41 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{41}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$ \n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n \n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈\\sqrt{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.896)\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247324 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246764 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247324 1069 "q-112960" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247324 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247324 1071 "For the polynomial $f(x)=6{x}^{3}-x-2,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247324 1072 #uuid "ffad6985-2e65-5b9b-963d-3e47210789f1" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247324 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 2.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{6}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{2}{3}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{6},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{3},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247325 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246735 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247325 1069 "q-112964" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247325 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247325 1071 "For the polynomial $f(x)=20{x}^{5}-7{x}^{4}+x-4,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247325 1072 #uuid "86a67078-b75c-5a5d-bcb6-0d3741c7e938" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247325 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-4,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2$ and $\\pm 4.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $20,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 4,$ $\\pm 5,$ $\\pm 10$ and $\\pm 20.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{10}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{20}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{10}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{20}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{10}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to the lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{10}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{20}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{10}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{4}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{5}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{20},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{10},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{5},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{5},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $20$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 10=20.$)" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247326 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246918 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247326 1069 "q-112966" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247326 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247326 1071 "For the polynomial $f(x)=7{x}^{8}-9{x}^{4}-2x+14,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247326 1072 #uuid "4724f0e9-cc95-5d38-b250-becbfe09ba88" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247326 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $14,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 7$ and $\\pm 14.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $7,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 7.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{7}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{7}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{7}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{14}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{14}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to the lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm \\frac{2}{7}, \\\\ \\pm 7, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 14, & \\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{7},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{7},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 7,\\,\\pm 14.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $12$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 6=12.$)" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246907 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247327 1069 "q-112984" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247327 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247327 1071 "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+12x+4$ has a root in the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},0),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247327 1072 #uuid "617dc73e-e9d7-5bc2-a43f-31dc7d4b171b" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247327 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $4$ and the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }4}{\\text{factor of }4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},0)$ is $-\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nUsing the synthetic division, we get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $4$ | $-15$ | $12$ | $4$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $4$ | $-16$ | $16$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{1}{4})(4{x}^{2}-16x+16).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{1}{4})(4{x}^{2}-16x+16) \\\\  & =4(x+\\frac{1}{4})({x}^{2}-4x+4) \\\\  & =(4x+1)(x-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-\\frac{1}{4},2.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{4}+2=\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247328 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246990 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247328 1069 "q-112989" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247328 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247328 1071 "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-2x+1$ has a root in the interval $(\\frac{1}{4},1),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247328 1072 #uuid "5ec4e979-d834-5890-b36a-49ae78aa8863" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247328 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $1$ and the leading coefficient is $8.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }1}{\\text{factor of }8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(\\frac{1}{4},1)$ is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nUsing the synthetic division, we get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $8$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $0$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $8$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{1}{2})(8{x}^{2}-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{1}{2})(8{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =2(x-\\frac{1}{2})(4{x}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =(2x-1)(2x+1)(2x-1) \\\\  & =(2x+1)(2x-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247329 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246859 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247329 1069 "q-11301" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247329 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247329 1071 "Find the area bounded between the curve $x=-4{y}^{2}+12,$ the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247329 1072 #uuid "e08978bb-2bee-5e6d-b61b-463f0c2a6908" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247329 1074 "To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4{y}^{2}+12)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+12{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-4\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}+12\\cdot y{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-4(\\frac{1}{3}-0)+12(1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}+12 \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247330 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246890 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247330 1069 "q-11311" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247330 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247330 1071 "Find the area of the closed region between the curve $x=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-y$ and the $y$-axis." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247330 1072 #uuid "2f6c28ca-af61-5213-9ff6-76a1753ee8ae" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247330 1074 "First, we find $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-y & =0 \\\\ y(\\frac{1}{2}y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region.\n\n![](images/29/29cf73562152cb8201c8e93545ca7f8416aa8412217cfc9dcdaf87e5b794d971.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-y$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=0$ to $y=2.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}-{\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(2)^{3}}{3}-0)-(\\frac{(2)^{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246884 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247331 1069 "q-113115" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247331 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247331 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ \nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to S}$ from $B$ to $S$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247331 1072 #uuid "aa182112-8f71-5fbc-8bf6-38e37fb6643c" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247331 1074 "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors of $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247332 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246987 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247332 1069 "q-113169" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247332 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247332 1071 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{3{x}^{4}-x+3}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247332 1072 #uuid "e6b2e6ea-5cee-5240-b8ad-ecb186345e79" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247332 1074 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{3{x}^{4}-x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}{(3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}$ and $\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}{(3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0-0}{3-0+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247333 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246659 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247333 1069 "q-113173" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247333 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247333 1071 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{7x+3}{6{x}^{7}-2{x}^{2}-1}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247333 1072 #uuid "6c3d325e-4c3f-5fb9-a295-31ae3dfeab70" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247333 1074 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{7}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{7},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{7x+3}{6{x}^{7}-2{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7x}{{x}^{7}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{7}}{{x}^{7}}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{7}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{{x}^{6}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}})}{(6-\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{7}{{x}^{6}},\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}},\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{{x}^{6}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{7}})}{(6-\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{6-0-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247334 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246573 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247334 1069 "q-113178" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247334 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247334 1071 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}-3x+2}{{x}^{2}+1}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247334 1072 #uuid "cd2ccdee-7055-5d1d-8ba2-f6170151c1c9" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247334 1074 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{4}-3x+2}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{x},$ $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{{x}^{2}-0+0}{1+0} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247335 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246718 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247335 1069 "q-113183" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247335 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247335 1071 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{8{x}^{6}+3{x}^{2}-7}{{x}^{4}-6x}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247335 1072 #uuid "dfc0eb92-e1dc-57f8-af5d-fa33fa1e76f4" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247335 1074 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{4}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{8{x}^{6}+3{x}^{2}-7}{{x}^{4}-6x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8{x}^{6}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{6x}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(8{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})}{(1-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}},$ $\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}}$ and $\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(8{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})}{(1-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{8{x}^{2}+0-0}{1-0} \\\\  & =8{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247336 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246576 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247336 1069 "q-113214" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247336 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247336 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{5g+3h}{3h}-\\frac{7g-2h}{3h}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247336 1072 #uuid "820302ba-7daa-5842-ae37-80ccb4043de0" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247336 1074 "We have a common denominator of $3h,$ so we add the numerators and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5g+3h}{3h}-\\frac{7g-2h}{3h} & = \\\\ \\frac{5g+3h-(7g-2h)}{3h} & = \\\\ \\frac{5g+3h-7g+2h}{3h} & = \\\\ \\frac{5h-2g}{3h}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246649 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247337 1069 "q-113217" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247337 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247337 1071 "Write $3+\\frac{5-p}{p}$ as a single fraction." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247337 1072 #uuid "db256712-a567-5c31-aac9-855b317092f8" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247337 1074 "Using the fact that $3=\\frac{3}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}+\\frac{5-p}{p}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $p$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{1}+\\frac{5-p}{p} & = \\\\ \\frac{p}{p}\\cdot \\frac{3}{1}+\\frac{5-p}{p} & = \\\\ \\frac{3p}{p}+\\frac{5-p}{p}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3p}{p}+\\frac{5-p}{p} & = \\\\ \\frac{3p+5-p}{p} & = \\\\ \\frac{2p+5}{p}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246802 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247338 1069 "q-113218" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247338 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247338 1071 "$2-\\frac{(-6)}{t}=$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247338 1072 #uuid "6a165a7a-a9ab-5ae7-96ca-f3a6cb8eeab0" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247338 1074 "Using the fact that $2=\\frac{2}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{1}-\\frac{(-6)}{t}=.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $t$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{1}-\\frac{(-6)}{t} & = \\\\ \\frac{t}{t}\\cdot \\frac{2}{1}-\\frac{(-6)}{t} & = \\\\ \\frac{2t}{t}-\\frac{(-6)}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2t}{t}-\\frac{(-6)}{t} & = \\\\ \\frac{2t-(-6)}{t} & = \\\\ \\frac{2t+6}{t} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(t+3)}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247339 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246909 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247339 1069 "q-113219" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247339 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247339 1071 "Write $5+\\frac{h-3}{h}$ as a single fraction." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247339 1072 #uuid "896043de-6bd3-58d0-93eb-3b6444623304" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247339 1074 "Using the fact that $5=\\frac{5}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{1}+\\frac{h-3}{h}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $h$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $h:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{1}+\\frac{h-3}{h} & = \\\\ \\frac{h}{h}\\cdot \\frac{5}{1}+\\frac{h-3}{h} & = \\\\ \\frac{5h}{h}+\\frac{h-3}{h}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5h}{h}+\\frac{h-3}{h} & = \\\\ \\frac{5h+h-3}{h} & = \\\\ \\frac{6h-3}{h} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(2h-1)}{h}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247340 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246896 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247340 1069 "q-113221" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247340 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247340 1071 "Write $6+\\frac{3-2k}{k}$ as a single fraction." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247340 1072 #uuid "d25e0ccf-d3b4-58e5-809a-e678b308dab7" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247340 1074 "Using the fact that $6=\\frac{6}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{1}+\\frac{3-2k}{k}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $k$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{1}+\\frac{3-2k}{k} & = \\\\ \\frac{k}{k}\\cdot \\frac{6}{1}+\\frac{3-2k}{k} & = \\\\ \\frac{6k}{k}+\\frac{3-2k}{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6k}{k}+\\frac{3-2k}{k} & = \\\\ \\frac{6k+3-2k}{k} & = \\\\ \\frac{4k+3}{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247341 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246908 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247341 1069 "q-113225" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247341 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247341 1071 "Write $\\frac{3w}{w+4}-6$ as a single fraction." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247341 1072 #uuid "0d456d55-d203-5b62-816e-e770a13ebca5" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247341 1074 "Using the fact that $6=\\frac{6}{1}$, we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{3w}{w+4}-\\frac{6}{1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $w+4$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the second fraction by $w+4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3w}{w+4}-\\frac{6}{1} & = \\\\ \\frac{3w}{w+4}-\\frac{w+4}{w+4}\\cdot \\frac{6}{1} & = \\\\ \\frac{3w}{w+4}-\\frac{6(w+4)}{w+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3w}{w+4}-\\frac{6(w+4)}{w+4} & = \\\\ \\frac{3w-6(w+4)}{w+4} & = \\\\ \\frac{3w-6w-24}{w+4} & = \\\\ \\frac{-3w-24}{w+4} & = \\\\ -\\frac{3(w+8)}{w+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247342 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246923 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247342 1069 "q-11323" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247342 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247342 1071 "![](images/36/36aaf58af536428c74cdb8f4c02c2b4f07e414422e8aea378c75d7abcff58cff.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{YX}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{XV}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{VZ}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{YW}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{XW}$ in terms of $a,b,c,$ and $d.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247342 1072 #uuid "4193f1f9-5dd6-5e42-ab53-c9d471fede26" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247342 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $X$ to $W$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$  \n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{XW}=\\overset{\\to }{XY}+\\overset{\\to }{YW},\n$$\n\nas shown below. \n\n![](images/47/4758b51fd3dc076059d60e944de0ad8375bb304240365959d4c6cae94f95a535.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{XY}=-a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{YW}=d.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/e9/e942d61c1f51c36dc6cd04f88ff5e37f538920a34c1af5bc780c47691567b668.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{XW} & =\\overset{\\to }{XY}+\\overset{\\to }{YW} \\\\  & =-a+d \\\\  & =d-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247343 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246565 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247343 1069 "q-113253" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247343 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247343 1071 "What are the solutions of the equation $(x-1)^{2}=-64?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247343 1072 #uuid "d661d662-98e0-5402-85ce-5aae28fdb450" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247343 1074 "The left-hand side is a perfect square. Therefore, we can solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =-64 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-64} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 8\\,\\text{i} \\\\ x & =1\\pm 8\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=1+8\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=1-8\\,\\text{i}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247344 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246865 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247344 1069 "q-113291" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247344 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247344 1071 "Given that $\\frac{5n+3}{n-2}=b$ and $\\frac{2n-1}{2-n}=c,$ what is $b-c?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247344 1072 #uuid "66bb3bb0-0115-554d-a6a3-f538c995da20" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247344 1074 "We wish to subtract the following fractions:\n\n$$\nb-c=\\frac{5n+3}{n-2}-\\frac{2n-1}{2-n}\n$$\n\nIn order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $n-2$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5n+3}{n-2}-\\frac{2n-1}{2-n} & = \\\\ \\frac{5n+3}{n-2}-\\frac{(-1)}{(-1)}\\cdot \\frac{2n-1}{2-n} & = \\\\ \\frac{5n+3}{n-2}-\\frac{-2n+1}{n-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{(5n+3)-(-2n+1)}{n-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{5n+3+2n-1}{n-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{7n+2}{n-2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247345 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246691 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247345 1069 "q-113296" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247345 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247345 1071 "Given that $\\frac{7v}{2(3+2v)}=t$ and $\\frac{3-5v}{3+2v}=r,$ what is $t+r?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247345 1072 #uuid "77efbc7d-72cb-5364-b916-b8865a568a34" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247345 1074 "We wish to add the following fractions:\n\n$$\nt+r=\\frac{7v}{2(3+2v)}+\\frac{3-5v}{3+2v}\n$$\n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $2(3+2v)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7v}{2(3+2v)}+\\frac{3-5v}{3+2v} & = \\\\ \\frac{7v}{2(3+2v)}+\\frac{2}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3-5v}{3+2v} & = \\\\ \\frac{7v}{2(3+2v)}+\\frac{2(3-5v)}{2(3+2v)} & = \\\\ \\frac{7v+2(3-5v)}{2(3+2v)} & = \\\\ \\frac{7v+6-10v}{2(3+2v)} & = \\\\ \\frac{6-3v}{2(3+2v)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(2-v)}{2(3+2v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247346 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246847 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247346 1069 "q-113303" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247346 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247346 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{5}{2-h}-\\frac{3}{g(2-h)}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247346 1072 #uuid "d446f78d-7f19-5904-b8b0-ee54e70de00c" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247346 1074 "In order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $g(2-h)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $g:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2-h}-\\frac{3}{g(2-h)} & = \\\\ \\frac{g}{g}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2-h}-\\frac{3}{g(2-h)} & = \\\\ \\frac{5g}{g(2-h)}-\\frac{3}{g(2-h)} & = \\\\ \\frac{5g-3}{g(2-h)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247347 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246667 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247347 1069 "q-113315" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247347 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247347 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{3}{c(5d+1)}-\\frac{5}{5d+1}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247347 1072 #uuid "ee962794-bfd5-55de-8444-a687faba72af" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247347 1074 "In order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $c(5d+1)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{c(5d+1)}-\\frac{5}{5d+1} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{c(5d+1)}-\\frac{c}{c}\\cdot \\frac{5}{5d+1} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{c(5d+1)}-\\frac{5c}{c(5d+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3-5c}{c(5d+1)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247348 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246672 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247348 1069 "q-113328" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247348 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247348 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{5}{3x}?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247348 1072 #uuid "241449fb-3074-516b-8678-a2bc1cb483a5" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247348 1074 "To plot $y=\\frac{5}{3x},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/a3/a32495fcca947d01cf8d3218fc5cfc0cce083a34e6fb6b629d92ba9e47039a6e.png)\n\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{5}{3}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{5}{3x}.$\n\n![](images/98/98920680f0cfd9aa1a6b6c1faacdbe6c23db39580083c42c8276612605c987ec.png)" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247349 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246603 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247349 1069 "q-113335" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247349 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247349 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{6x}?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247349 1072 #uuid "d7164c76-5483-59d4-a0d7-e2d3738d4ded" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247349 1074 "To plot $y=-\\frac{1}{6x},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/9d/9da0db2e6c97e507e82b7ca801e46802fabf91ad31ff53ad3cc9ae580151370a.png)\n\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{6}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{6x}.$\n\n![](images/71/7152303066f322a8179b2e37863cea6367290998e31382fa0470a1ab50b4d2ff.png)" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247350 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246657 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247350 1069 "q-113337" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247350 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247350 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-\\frac{5}{2x}?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247350 1072 #uuid "ffb268df-6810-52c3-8e35-47b785c8324e" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247350 1074 "To plot $y=-\\frac{5}{2x},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/d5/d5d93e12275c683e0a56f0ec9a0c99540a7774879cd0f8fa69151ddba68a44fa.png)\n\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{5}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\frac{5}{2x}.$\n\n![](images/3b/3bc730306eaa88729b46b20ddb32782c89186f6db66c51fd924b2622804396aa.png)" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247351 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246724 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247351 1069 "q-113339" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247351 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247351 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-\\frac{7}{3x}?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247351 1072 #uuid "b8d427d8-0893-5ac0-9ddb-b21a6f1227d0" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247351 1074 "To plot $y=-\\frac{7}{3x},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/d5/d5d93e12275c683e0a56f0ec9a0c99540a7774879cd0f8fa69151ddba68a44fa.png)\n\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{7}{3}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\frac{7}{3x}.$\n\n![](images/ed/ed5613c7614ab24b823ba0729ed1a19e2d0e57a4b81c0ba7868ccb61a52e7904.png)" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247352 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246739 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247352 1069 "q-113341" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247352 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247352 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-\\frac{5}{4x}?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247352 1072 #uuid "6c2e1962-873d-5fd3-ac7c-344af43753ef" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247352 1074 "To plot $y=-\\frac{5}{4x},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/d5/d5d93e12275c683e0a56f0ec9a0c99540a7774879cd0f8fa69151ddba68a44fa.png)\n\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{5}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\frac{5}{4x}.$\n\n![](images/6e/6e56262645ef598cacb1377193398f1f3a59a2637193d05c813ffa075603c262.png)" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246671 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247353 1069 "q-113372" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247353 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{4}{5x}+3?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247353 1072 #uuid "097f9b36-1f3f-531e-bec1-feea7bb61a3f" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247353 1074 "To plot $y=\\frac{4}{5x}+1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/a3/a32495fcca947d01cf8d3218fc5cfc0cce083a34e6fb6b629d92ba9e47039a6e.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{4}{5}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{4}{5x}.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea9bd36b532b46d3edcc99497b77e75288499a4b02239f5d2416cff6a4259478.png)\n\n- Translate it by $3$ units up to get the graph of $y=\\frac{4}{5x}+3.$\n\n![](images/e6/e6798ba049ab7040eec51d2da525d3383816578423e11384ced1f3665a2e50fd.png)" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246711 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247354 1069 "q-113411" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247354 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247354 1071 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $C$ to the basis $B$ is given by\n${P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247354 1072 #uuid "d86d7038-3836-547b-82af-27a9e8b49db8" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247354 1074 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{C\\to B}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C} & ={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246844 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247355 1069 "q-113413" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247355 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247355 1071 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $C$ to the basis $B$ is given by\n${P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$  ." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247355 1072 #uuid "6524c152-aeba-5139-803b-30e61293f48c" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247355 1074 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{C\\to B}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C} & ={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 9}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246814 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247356 1069 "q-113466" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247356 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-\\frac{2}{x-2}?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247356 1072 #uuid "181027b7-8f72-5b75-b19f-e9d026f6aac6" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247356 1074 "To plot $y=-\\frac{2}{x-2},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/9d/9da0db2e6c97e507e82b7ca801e46802fabf91ad31ff53ad3cc9ae580151370a.png)\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units to the right to get the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{x-2}.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c8dd0e4726747b00e4377e43c06693326e10db2977257c9c20da4953127ddfd.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\frac{2}{x-2}.$\n\n![](images/20/207dec9757b65a75c25ba7ace7a12bec8e922bda9018e58d6511d12de47426b7.png)" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247357 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246760 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247357 1069 "q-113476" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247357 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247357 1071 "![](images/c1/c1159b59ac6a52c570852cb47ec1ef07d0985ee8b734f2e375cf54919c8b537b.png)\n\nThe graph represents the function $y=\\frac{a}{x}+k.$ What is the value of $a+k?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247357 1072 #uuid "67f38dd1-2470-5551-aa5d-9c22a7b1ef76" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247357 1074 "First, notice that the horizontal asymptote of the graph is $y=1.5.$ This means that $k=1.5$ and we can re-write our function as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{a}{x}+1.5.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are given that the point $(1,3.5)$ lies on the graph. Substituting its coordinates into the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{a}{x}+1.5 \\\\ 3.5 & =\\frac{a}{1}+1.5 \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{a}{1} \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+k=2+1.5=3.5.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246811 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247358 1069 "q-113478" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247358 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247358 1071 "![](images/4f/4f8e1c3db4120b0fcd8491a850d238b81452fb0294145a2062f96c4655164a04.png)\n\nThe graph represents the function $y=\\frac{a}{x}+k.$ What is the value of $a+k?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247358 1072 #uuid "cb7f3096-38d4-5d98-8011-5cb0fe69482f" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247358 1074 "First, notice that the horizontal asymptote of the graph is $y=-1.5.$ This means that $k=-1.5$ and we can re-write our function as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{a}{x}-1.5.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are given that the point $(1,-2)$ lies on the graph. Substituting its coordinates into the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{a}{x}-1.5 \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{a}{1}-1.5 \\\\ -0.5 & =\\frac{a}{1} \\\\ a & =-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+k=-0.5-1.5=-2.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247359 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246803 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247359 1069 "q-113482" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247359 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247359 1071 "![](images/4d/4d2dec8a05bc9835693c1df9dbc54618fd39d77667cbbdcd5bb6d92bf25c0d97.png)\n\nThe graph represents the function $y=\\frac{a}{x-b}+k.$ What is the value of $a+b+k?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247359 1072 #uuid "3d881fc0-905f-5d7f-8a88-250eeb9291b8" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247359 1074 "First, notice that the horizontal asymptote of the graph is $y=0.5,$ while the vertical asymptote is $x=3.$ This means that $k=0.5$ and $b=3,$ and we can re-write our function as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{a}{x-3}+0.5.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are given that the point $(1,1.5)$ lies on the graph. Substituting its coordinates into the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{a}{x-3}+0.5 \\\\ 1.5 & =\\frac{a}{1-3}+0.5 \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{a}{-2} \\\\ a & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+b+k=-2+3+0.5=1.5.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246572 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247360 1069 "q-113486" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247360 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247360 1071 "$\\int 48(3x-2)^{7}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247360 1072 #uuid "b3a2c849-8914-5088-a188-98bfa5cab912" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247360 1074 "Let $u=3x-2.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 48(3x-2)^{7}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 48{u}^{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =48\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\int {u}^{7}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{8}}{8}+C \\\\  & =2\\cdot {u}^{8}+C \\\\  & =2(3x-2)^{8}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[2(3x-2)^{8}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[2(3x-2)^{8}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =16(3x-2)^{7}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x-2)+0 \\\\  & =16(3x-2)^{7}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =48(3x-2)^{7}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247361 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246899 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247361 1069 "q-113492" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247361 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247361 1071 "$\\int 6(5-3x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247361 1072 #uuid "b151b1a4-88b3-56e5-8da5-20d1f21f5f65" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247361 1074 "Let $u=5-3x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6(5-3x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6{u}^{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\int {u}^{2}\\cdot \\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\int {u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(5-3x)^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{2}{3}(5-3x)^{3}+C] & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{2}{3}(5-3x)^{3}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-2(5-3x)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5-3x)+0 \\\\  & =-2(5-3x)^{2}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6(5-3x)^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247362 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246975 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247362 1069 "q-113494" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247362 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247362 1071 "The domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2x+n}$ is $(-\\infty ,3)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$ Find the value of $n.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247362 1072 #uuid "e3de75c7-c408-5af5-a1ca-4557383f13e8" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247362 1074 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+n & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-n \\\\ x & =-\\frac{n}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=-\\frac{n}{2}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=3$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $-\\frac{n}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{n}{2} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{n}{2} & =-3 \\\\ n & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247363 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246666 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247363 1069 "q-113497" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247363 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247363 1071 "The domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{4}{kx-3}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{5})\\cup (\\frac{3}{5},\\infty ).$ What is the value of $k?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247363 1072 #uuid "fdf85541-e5ed-5dfe-8229-d79a5f290bfb" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247363 1074 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx-3 & =0 \\\\ kx & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=\\frac{3}{k}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=\\frac{3}{5}$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $\\frac{3}{k},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{k} & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ k & =\\frac{3\\cdot 5}{3} \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247364 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246720 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247364 1069 "q-113503" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247364 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247364 1071 "$\\int 9(3-2x)^{8}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247364 1072 #uuid "4aa132c6-373a-5f63-a95e-dc8b634b917a" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247364 1074 "Let $u=3-2x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 9(3-2x)^{8}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 9{u}^{8}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\int {u}^{8}\\cdot \\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}\\int {u}^{8}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{9}}{9}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{9}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3-2x)^{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{2}(3-2x)^{9}+C] & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{2}(3-2x)^{9}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}(3-2x)^{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3-2x)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}(3-2x)^{8}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =9(3-2x)^{8}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247365 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246593 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247365 1069 "q-113510" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247365 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247365 1071 "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}$ and $C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247365 1072 #uuid "3b028c1b-2596-51e4-85c0-5cc2ad0dbfbc" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247365 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 9 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246786 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247366 1069 "q-113511" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247366 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247366 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{1}{(5x-1)^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247366 1072 #uuid "5a85bee8-3587-5c69-bd5f-2ededc7e8236" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247366 1074 "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (5x-1)^{-6}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5x-1.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5x-1)^{-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{-6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{-6}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5}){u}^{-5}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{25{u}^{5}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{25(5x-1)^{5}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{25(5x-1)^{5}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{25(5x-1)^{5}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{25}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(5x-1)^{-5}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{25}\\cdot (-5)\\cdot (5x-1)^{-6}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x-1)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5(5x-1)^{6}}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5x-1)^{6}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246570 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247367 1069 "q-113512" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247367 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247367 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{1}{(2x+3)^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247367 1072 #uuid "094b9ad8-d309-510f-9910-b086cac9a8d2" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247367 1074 "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (2x+3)^{-7}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x+3.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2x+3)^{-7}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{-7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{-7}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6}){u}^{-6}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12{u}^{6}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12(2x+3)^{6}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{12(2x+3)^{6}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{12(2x+3)^{6}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(2x+3)^{-6}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-6)\\cdot (2x+3)^{-7}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x+3)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(2x+3)^{7}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(2x+3)^{7}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246835 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247368 1069 "q-113515" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247368 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247368 1071 "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247368 1072 #uuid "cb921b26-a20c-5f23-a094-49ac99b2c6bd" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247368 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{e}_{1},{e}_{2}}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{17}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{4}{17} & \\frac{1}{17} & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{4}{17} & \\frac{1}{17} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{17} & -\\frac{4}{17} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=17{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{4}{17} & \\frac{1}{17} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{4}{17}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 17\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 17\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246582 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247369 1069 "q-113520" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247369 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247369 1071 "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}$ and $C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247369 1072 #uuid "ba537c3c-c2ae-5289-808d-7f1890eac09c" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247369 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246995 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247370 1069 "q-113525" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247370 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247370 1071 "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+7x}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247370 1072 #uuid "163eeae3-71ad-5b66-a0aa-4b05d346c12b" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247370 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+7x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{x({x}^{2}+x+7)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx({x}^{2}+x+7)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x=0$ gives the solution $x=0.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+x+7=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-4(1)(7) \\\\  & =1-28 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=0.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246955 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247371 1069 "q-113532" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247371 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247371 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247371 1072 #uuid "a3442786-8423-543c-a685-0f42805b9492" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247371 1074 "First, let $u=2x+3.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|+C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|2x+3|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|2x+3|).\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247372 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246726 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247372 1069 "q-113533" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247372 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247372 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247372 1072 #uuid "3fb614fb-4ec0-5ed7-b971-448980a19e29" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247372 1074 "First, let $u=3x+4.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+C & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|).\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246560 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247373 1069 "q-113537" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247373 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247373 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{4}{5x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247373 1072 #uuid "9c5750cc-deef-5876-b8ba-af3969d07cbe" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247373 1074 "First, let $u=5x+3.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{5x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int \\frac{1}{5x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+3|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+3|+C & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+3|+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+3|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5x+3|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{4}{5x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5x+3|).\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247374 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246708 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247374 1069 "q-113538" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247374 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247374 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{3}{1-4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247374 1072 #uuid "55bd9be4-7ed0-5405-bc5b-6c0d4ac3a0a2" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247374 1074 "First, let $u=1-4x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{1-4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-4x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-4x|+C & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-4x|-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-4x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|1-4x|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{3}{1-4x}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|1-4x|).\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246715 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247375 1069 "q-113540" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247375 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247375 1071 "Calculate $\\int (\\frac{1}{3x+2}+\\frac{1}{3x-2})\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247375 1072 #uuid "8007e479-04c9-545e-b360-e628f7f99f22" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247375 1074 "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{3x+2}+\\frac{1}{3x-2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(3x+2)(3x-2)|+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(3x+2)(3x-2)|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246973 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247376 1069 "q-113544" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247376 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247376 1071 "Calculate $\\int (\\frac{1}{x+3}+\\frac{1}{x-3})\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247376 1072 #uuid "7c3ea51c-8b14-5b7a-ba99-899972c3822c" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247376 1074 "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{x+3}+\\frac{1}{x-3})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+3)(x-3)|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(x+3)(x-3)|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247377 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246924 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247377 1069 "q-113545" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247377 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247377 1071 "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247377 1072 #uuid "daec1f65-9c08-5df4-939b-bebb2395a749" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247377 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}({x}^{2}+x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}+x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+x+2=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =1-4(1)(2) \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247378 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246661 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247378 1069 "q-113556" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247378 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247378 1071 "$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is given above. Find $[x]_{C}$ given that $x=2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}+4{b}_{3}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247378 1072 #uuid "e92b7de6-a8a6-53cb-aa1a-a2e89d07afe1" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247378 1074 "First, notice that since $x=2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}+4{b}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247379 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246858 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247379 1069 "q-113557" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247379 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247379 1071 "$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is given above. Find $[x]_{C}$ given that $x=5{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}-{b}_{3}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247379 1072 #uuid "b0aa2b6d-5bc4-5e57-a220-576badff809b" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247379 1074 "First, notice that since $x=5{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}-{b}_{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247380 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246961 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247380 1069 "q-11357" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247380 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247380 1071 "Evaluate $\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+17}{({x}^{2}+7)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247380 1072 #uuid "9a441ef3-f695-504a-99f7-e8e548e9853f" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247380 1074 "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+7$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+17}{({x}^{2}+7)(x+1)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+7}+\\frac{C}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+7)(x+1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x+17 & =(Ax+B)(x+1)+C({x}^{2}+7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=-1,B=3,$ and $C=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+17}{({x}^{2}+7)(x+1)}=\\frac{-x+3}{{x}^{2}+7}+\\frac{2}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+17}{({x}^{2}+7)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{-x+3}{{x}^{2}+7}+\\frac{2}{x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+7}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+7}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use a substitution $u={x}^{2}+7.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+7}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+7| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+7}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}}).\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+17}{({x}^{2}+7)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+7}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+7}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+7)+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}})+2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+7)+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{7}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{7}})+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247381 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246650 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247381 1069 "q-113581" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247381 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247381 1071 "Calculate $\\int 12{x}^{5}\\sqrt{2{x}^{6}-3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247381 1072 #uuid "6fab21e9-9cee-52c0-ac1e-55235a31831f" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247381 1074 "We note that $12{x}^{5}$ is the derivative of the quantity in the square root. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu=2{x}^{6}-3.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =12{x}^{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=12{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 12{x}^{5}\\sqrt{2{x}^{6}-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\sqrt{2{x}^{6}-3}\\cdot 12{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{(\\frac{3}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u=2{x}^{6}-3$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\int 12{x}^{5}\\sqrt{2{x}^{6}-3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{2(2{x}^{6}-3)^{3/2}}{3}+C.\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247382 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246983 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247382 1069 "q-11359" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247382 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247382 1071 "Evaluate $\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x+42}{({x}^{2}+9)(x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247382 1072 #uuid "d886c514-a76a-5fa4-982f-e0a9beeb72b9" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247382 1074 "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+9$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+x+42}{({x}^{2}+9)(x-3)}=\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+9}+\\frac{C}{x-3}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $({x}^{2}+9)(x-3),$ and we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+x+42=(Ax+B)(x-3)+C({x}^{2}+9).\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=-2,B=-5,$ and $C=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+x+42}{({x}^{2}+9)(x-3)}=-\\frac{2x+5}{{x}^{2}+9}+\\frac{3}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x+42}{({x}^{2}+9)(x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{2x+5}{{x}^{2}+9}+\\frac{3}{x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+9}\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+9}\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use a substitution $u={x}^{2}+9.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{u})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+9| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}).\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x+42}{({x}^{2}+9)(x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+9}\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+9}\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+9)-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+3\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+9)-\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247383 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246850 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247383 1069 "q-113600" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247383 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247383 1071 "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247383 1072 #uuid "ca416b56-0c6d-5fb5-8b76-d2124cfd2e38" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247383 1074 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3} & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3} & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247384 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246622 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247384 1069 "q-113604" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247384 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247384 1071 "Which expression is missing in the following equation?\n\n$$\n(\\frac{2}{1})=(\\frac{1}{0})+[]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247384 1072 #uuid "677a09e1-8d23-5bcc-a6cc-3c545a04ef8b" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247384 1074 "To find $(\\frac{2}{1}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $2$ and position $1$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $(\\frac{2}{1})$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{0}{0}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{1}{0}) &  & (\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{2}{0}) &  & (\\frac{2}{1}) &  & (\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\  &  &  &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(\\frac{2}{1})$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n(\\frac{2}{1})=(\\frac{1}{0})+(\\frac{1}{1})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $(\\frac{1}{1}).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247385 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246588 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247385 1069 "q-113606" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247385 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247385 1071 "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247385 1072 #uuid "162a4f62-2177-5623-998d-5746dbddde07" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247385 1074 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 6-(-7)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{5}{9}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{5}{9}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247386 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246807 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247386 1069 "q-113607" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247386 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247386 1071 "Which expression is missing in the following equation?\n\n$$\n(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3})=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2})+[]\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247386 1072 #uuid "14159827-676f-5696-940b-0d1eff42fff0" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247386 1074 "To find $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $6$ and position $3$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3})$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{0}{0}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{1}{0}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{1}{1}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1}) &  & (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}