-5763-bfa6-f9be1199e7b5" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247393 1074 "The binomial coefficient $(\\frac{n}{r})$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n(\\frac{n}{r})=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $n=7$ and $r=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{7}{5}) & =\\frac{7!}{(7-5)!\\cdot 5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5!}{2!\\cdot 5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{2\\cdot \\cancel{5!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{42}{2} \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246700 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247394 1069 "q-113620" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247394 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247394 1071 "Determine the value of $(\\frac{7}{2}).$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247394 1072 #uuid "2c53882b-a66e-5878-876b-0012c351b8df" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247394 1074 "The binomial coefficient $(\\frac{n}{r})$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n(\\frac{n}{r})=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $n=7$ and $r=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\frac{7!}{(7-2)!\\cdot 2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5!}{5!\\cdot 2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{\\cancel{5!}\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{42}{2} \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246852 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247395 1069 "q-113621" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247395 1071 "The coefficient of ${x}^{18}$ in the expansion of ${(2+{x}^{3})}^{10}$ is" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247395 1072 #uuid "521595db-fa9d-5190-ad38-7460533af950" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247395 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(2+{x}^{3})}^{10},$ we have $a=2,$ $b={x}^{3},$ and $n=10.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+{x}^{3})}^{10}=(\\frac{10}{0}){2}^{10}{({x}^{3})}^{0}+(\\frac{10}{1}){2}^{10-1}{({x}^{3})}^{1}+(\\frac{10}{2}){2}^{10-2}{({x}^{3})}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{10}{10}){2}^{10-10}{({x}^{3})}^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${x}^{18}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{10}{6}){2}^{10-6}{({x}^{3})}^{6}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{10}{6}){2}^{10-6}{({x}^{3})}^{6} & =210\\cdot {2}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{18} \\\\  & =210\\cdot 16\\cdot {x}^{18} \\\\  & =3\\,360{x}^{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{18}$ is $3\\,360.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246637 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247396 1069 "q-113624" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247396 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247396 1071 "The coefficient of ${z}^{9}{x}^{3}$ in the expansion of ${(z+4x)}^{12}$ is" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247396 1072 #uuid "e5f29c08-3730-5777-b328-01f60419940a" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247396 1073 true 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247396 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(z+4x)}^{12},$ we have $a=z,$ $b=4x,$ and $n=12.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z+4x)}^{12}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{0}){z}^{12}(4x)^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{1}){z}^{12-1}(4x)^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{2}){z}^{12-2}(4x)^{2}+⋯+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{12}){z}^{12-12}(4x)^{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${z}^{9}{x}^{3}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{3}){z}^{12-3}(4x)^{3}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{3}){z}^{12-3}(4x)^{3} & =220{z}^{9}\\cdot {4}^{3}{x}^{3} \\\\  & =220{z}^{9}\\cdot 64{x}^{3} \\\\  & =14\\,080{z}^{9}{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${z}^{9}{x}^{3}$ is $14\\,080.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246921 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247397 1069 "q-113625" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247397 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247397 1071 "The coefficient of ${y}^{6}{z}^{3}$ in the expansion of $(y+3z)^{9}$ is" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247397 1072 #uuid "a27fbc12-981f-5945-a8a6-63ae6f519b73" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247397 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(y+3z)}^{9},$ we have $a=y,$ $b=3z,$ and $n=9.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(y+3z)}^{9}=(\\frac{9}{0}){y}^{9}(3z)^{0}+(\\frac{9}{1}){y}^{9-1}(3z)^{1}+(\\frac{9}{2}){y}^{9-2}(3z)^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{9}{9}){y}^{9-9}(3z)^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${y}^{6}{z}^{3}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{9}{3}){y}^{9-3}(3z)^{3}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{9}{3}){y}^{9-3}(3z)^{3} & =84{y}^{6}\\cdot {3}^{3}{z}^{3} \\\\  & =84{y}^{6}\\cdot 27{z}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\,268{y}^{6}{z}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${y}^{6}{z}^{3}$ is $2\\,268.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246964 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247398 1069 "q-113628" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247398 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247398 1071 "${(1+\\frac{c}{3})}^{6}=$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247398 1072 #uuid "17e9a773-d8fd-522c-9ebb-59672d321b3d" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247398 1074 "The first three terms of the binomial expansion for $(1+x)^{n}$ are given by\n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1)}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $x=\\frac{c}{3}$ and $n=6$ into the above formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+\\frac{c}{3})}^{6} & =1+6\\cdot \\frac{c}{3}+\\frac{6(6-1)}{2!}\\cdot {(\\frac{c}{3})}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+2c+\\frac{6\\cdot 5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{c}^{2}}{9}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+2c+15\\cdot \\frac{{c}^{2}}{9}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+2c+\\frac{5{c}^{2}}{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247399 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246986 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247399 1069 "q-113689" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247399 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247399 1071 "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247399 1072 #uuid "0e290de6-3133-56f5-894e-be4f887bce10" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247399 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 5 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -11 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 13 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -11 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}13 & 7 \\\\ -11 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 & 7 \\\\ -11 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247400 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246854 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247400 1069 "q-11369" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247400 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247400 1071 "$\\int \\frac{-9x+27}{(1-x)^{2}(x+5)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247400 1072 #uuid "973f7fe1-28bf-5387-838b-962550857c44" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247400 1074 "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{-9x+27}{(1-x)^{2}(x+5)}=\\frac{A}{(1-x)}+\\frac{B}{(1-x)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x+5)}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(1-x)^{2}(x+5)$ and get\n\n$$\n-9x+27=A(1-x)(x+5)+B(x+5)+C(1-x)^{2}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $C$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9(1)+27 & =A(1-1)(1+5)+B(1+5)+C(1-1)^{2} \\\\ -9+27 & =B(6) \\\\ B & =\\frac{18}{6} \\\\ B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-5,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9(-5)+27 & =A(1-(-5))(-5+5)+B(-5+5)+C(1-(-5))^{2} \\\\ -45+27 & =C{(6)}^{2} \\\\ C & =\\frac{72}{36} \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known values of $B$ and $C,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9(0)+27 & =A(1-0)(0+5)+B(0+5)+C(1-0)^{2} \\\\ 27 & =5A+5B+C \\\\ 27 & =5A+5(3)+2 \\\\ 27 & =5A+17 \\\\ 5A & =27-17 \\\\ A & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{-9x+27}{(1-x)^{2}(x+5)}=\\frac{2}{(1-x)}+\\frac{3}{(1-x)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(x+5)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{-9x+27}{(1-x)^{2}(x+5)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{(1-x)}+\\frac{3}{(1-x)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(x+5)})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x+5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-x|+\\frac{3}{1-x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+5|+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247401 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246705 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247401 1069 "q-113706" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247401 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247401 1071 "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247401 1072 #uuid "123986ad-90d2-58aa-b35b-26c97aa5670c" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247401 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ 5 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ 5 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247402 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246696 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247402 1069 "q-113710" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247402 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247402 1071 "Calculate $\\int 2{x}^{5}(4+{x}^{6})^{5}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247402 1072 #uuid "49e41322-d540-5688-b30a-e33aaf2dc9a7" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247402 1074 "In this case, the term $2{x}^{5}$ outside the parentheses isn't quite the derivative of $4+{x}^{6}.$ But the derivative is $6{x}^{5},$ so we're only out by a constant factor.\nSo we make the substitution\n\n$$\nu=4+{x}^{6}.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{5}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u=2{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can now write our integral in terms of $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{5}(4+{x}^{6})^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4+{x}^{6})^{5}\\cdot 2{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\int {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{6}}{6}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}{u}^{6}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}(4+{x}^{6})^{6}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{(4+{x}^{6})^{6}}{18}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247403 1068 #edb/ref 17592186247002 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247403 1069 "q-113711" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247403 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247403 1071 "Calculate $\\int {x}^{6}(2+{x}^{7})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247403 1072 #uuid "ddb8f392-6e2f-5096-a24e-dc20632151b9" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247403 1074 "In this case, the term ${x}^{6}$ outside the parentheses isn't quite the derivative of $2+{x}^{7}.$ But the derivative is $7{x}^{6},$ so we're only out by a constant factor.\nSo we make the substitution\n\n$$\nu=2+{x}^{7}.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =7{x}^{6}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{7}\\text{d}u={x}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can now write our integral in terms of $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{6}(2+{x}^{7})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2+{x}^{7})^{2}\\cdot {x}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\int {u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7}\\int {u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{21}{u}^{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{21}(2+{x}^{7})^{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{(2+{x}^{7})^{3}}{21}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247404 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246619 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247404 1069 "q-113715" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247404 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247404 1071 "Calculate $\\int 2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+10}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247404 1072 #uuid "289ac24b-4cc7-5baf-9d30-2e391bade4eb" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247404 1074 "In this case, the $2{x}^{2}$ that's outside the square root isn't quite the derivative of ${x}^{3}+10.$ But the derivative is $3{x}^{2},$ so we're only out by a constant factor.\n\nSo we make the substitution\n\n$$\nu={x}^{3}+10.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\text{d}u=2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can now write our integral in terms of $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+10}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\sqrt{{x}^{3}+10}\\cdot 2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{(\\frac{3}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {u}^{3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{({x}^{3}+10)^{3}}}{9}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246664 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247405 1069 "q-113719" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247405 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247405 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1-4{x}^{3}}{1+x-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247405 1072 #uuid "c5eb9147-51e3-5599-8ff3-2e89cfeea2e5" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247405 1074 "Let $u=1+x-{x}^{4}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1-4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=(1-4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1-4{x}^{3}}{1+x-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+x-{x}^{4}}\\cdot (1-4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+x-{x}^{4}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247406 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246722 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247406 1069 "q-113720" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247406 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247406 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{5{x}^{4}-1}{{x}^{5}-x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247406 1072 #uuid "6aa87c4a-351c-586b-be53-8401152f7ab3" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247406 1074 "Let $u={x}^{5}-x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5{x}^{4}-1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=(5{x}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5{x}^{4}-1}{{x}^{5}-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}-x}\\cdot (5{x}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{5}-x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247407 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246765 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247407 1069 "q-113721" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247407 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247407 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{2x+2}{{x}^{2}+2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247407 1072 #uuid "a444aaf3-15c5-5890-a26f-622f4c6bdbef" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247407 1074 "Let $u={x}^{2}+2x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x+2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=(2x+2)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+2}{{x}^{2}+2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2x}\\cdot (2x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+2x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247408 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246846 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247408 1069 "q-113726" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247408 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247408 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{7{x}^{4}}{{(3-{x}^{5})}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247408 1072 #uuid "14745f6d-a16a-5fbf-98d1-0322f61ed9f2" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247408 1074 "Let $u=3-{x}^{5}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-5{x}^{4}\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{7}{5}\\text{d}u=7{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7{x}^{4}}{{(3-{x}^{5})}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{(3-{x}^{5})}^{6}}\\cdot 7{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{6}}\\cdot (-\\frac{7}{5})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{5}(-\\frac{1}{5{u}^{5}})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{25{u}^{5}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{25(3-{x}^{5})^{5}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247409 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246801 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247409 1069 "q-113728" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247409 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247409 1071 "$\\int \\frac{2{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247409 1072 #uuid "b09729b9-ffc3-52fc-8706-830a753599b5" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247409 1074 "Let $u=1-{x}^{3}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,(-\\frac{2}{3})\\text{d}u=2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}}\\cdot 2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{3})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(2\\sqrt{u})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{1-{x}^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247410 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246609 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247410 1069 "q-11373" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247410 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247410 1071 "$\\int \\frac{14{x}^{2}+47x+42}{(2x+3)^{2}(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247410 1072 #uuid "ac06531e-6119-55f2-a664-c7ce48ffa7e1" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247410 1074 "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{14{x}^{2}+47x+42}{(2x+3)^{2}(x+3)}=\\frac{A}{(2x+3)}+\\frac{B}{(2x+3)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x+3)}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(2x+3)^{2}(x+3)$ and get\n\n$$\n14{x}^{2}+47x+42=A(2x+3)(x+3)+B(x+3)+C(2x+3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{3}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and $C$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14{(-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+47(-\\frac{3}{2})+42 & =A(2(-\\frac{3}{2})+3)(-\\frac{3}{2}+3) \\\\  & +B(-\\frac{3}{2}+3)+C{(2(-\\frac{3}{2})+3)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{63}{2}-\\frac{141}{2}+42 & =B(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{63-141+84}{2} & =B(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\ B & =-\\frac{12}{6} \\\\ B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14(-3)^{2}+47(-3)+42 & =A(2(-3)+3)(-3+3)+B(-3+3)+C(2(-3)+3)^{2} \\\\ 126-141+42 & =C{(-3)}^{2} \\\\ C & =\\frac{27}{9} \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known values of $B$ and $C,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14({0}^{2})+47(0)+42 & =A(2(0)+3)(0+3)+B(0+3)+C(2(0)+3)^{2} \\\\ 42 & =9A+3B+9C \\\\ 42 & =9A+3(2)+9(3) \\\\ 42 & =9A+33 \\\\ 9A & =42-33 \\\\ A & =\\frac{9}{9} \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{14{x}^{2}+47x+42}{(2x+3)^{2}(x+3)}=\\frac{1}{(2x+3)}+\\frac{2}{(2x+3)^{2}}+\\frac{3}{(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{14{x}^{2}+47x+42}{(2x+3)^{2}(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{(2x+3)}+\\frac{2}{(2x+3)^{2}}+\\frac{3}{(x+3)})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{(2x+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|-\\frac{1}{2x+3}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247411 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246886 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247411 1069 "q-113743" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247411 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247411 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{d}y.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247411 1072 #uuid "1e9f7c60-e13f-56df-879c-69f076975c52" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247411 1074 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int {y}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot y+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}y+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247412 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246959 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247412 1069 "q-113747" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247412 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247412 1071 "Calculate the definite integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{3{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247412 1072 #uuid "4f496a56-4bd2-5cbc-b0fa-ae68e7d900e8" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247412 1074 "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =3{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =6x \\\\ \\text{d}u & =6x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u & =x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=3{x}^{2}+1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u){|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247413 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246770 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247413 1069 "q-113750" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247413 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247413 1071 "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=12x+9{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3}$ have a local maximum?" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247413 1072 #uuid "2776d562-24a8-5f0b-bcac-9739cc2c4ba2" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247413 1074 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12+18x-12{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-6(2{x}^{2}-3x-2) \\\\  & =-6(x-2)(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-6(x-2)(2x+1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2},2.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,0,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-6(-1-2)(2(-1)+1)=-18<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =-6(0-2)(2(0)+1)=12>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-6(3-2)(2(3)+1)=-42<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2})$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $0$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=2$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local maximum at $x=2.$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247414 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246942 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247414 1069 "q-113751" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247414 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247414 1071 "Given that $u=x+1,$ which integral is equivalent to $\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247414 1072 #uuid "5e1a79b4-288f-5893-a2f8-463fb8d4ce10" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247414 1074 "Since $u=x+1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe also note that \n$x=u-1.$\n\nNow we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{(u-1)^{2}}{\\sqrt{u}}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int ({u}^{2}-2u+1){u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int ({u}^{3/2}-2{u}^{1/2}+{u}^{-1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int (\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}-2\\sqrt{u}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}})\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247415 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246555 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247415 1069 "q-113756" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247415 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247415 1071 "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+4,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is a local maximum\n- $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a local minimum" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247415 1072 #uuid "4af7007e-9d89-5ab4-8e7e-891076eaef01" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247415 1074 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}-2x \\\\  & =2x(3x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(3x-1)\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,\\frac{1}{4},1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)(3(-1)-1)=8>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-\\frac{1}{4}) & =2(\\frac{1}{4})[3(\\frac{1}{4})-1]=-\\frac{1}{8}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =2(1)(3(1)-1)=4>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\frac{1}{3})$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $(\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{1}{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247416 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246788 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247416 1069 "q-113758" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247416 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247416 1071 "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is a local minimum\n- $x=\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local minimum" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247416 1072 #uuid "04886f18-ebcf-50be-b62d-6d531bb447d4" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247416 1074 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}+10x \\\\  & =2x(3x+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(3x+5)\\,⟹\\,x=0,-\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=-\\frac{5}{3}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =2(-2)(3(-2)+5)=4>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)(3(-1)+5)=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =2(1)(3(1)+5)=16>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3})$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $(-\\frac{5}{3},0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{5}{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247417 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246979 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247417 1069 "q-113762" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247417 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247417 1071 "Given the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-3x+5,$ which of the following statements are true?" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247417 1072 #uuid "5562b548-f8da-5103-bcc1-290579039c5e" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247417 1074 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-3{x}^{2}+6x-3 \\\\  & =-3({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\  & =-3(x-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-3(x-1)^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nSo $x=1$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-3(0-1)^{2}=-3<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-3(2-1)^{2}=-3<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=1.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=1$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246693 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247418 1069 "q-113763" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247418 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247418 1071 "Given the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+3x-2,$ which of the following statements are true?" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247418 1072 #uuid "fd95aa30-1ca2-5a62-b658-637d3382deaf" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247418 1074 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+6x+3 \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+2x+1) \\\\  & =3(x+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n3(x+1)^{2}=0\\,⇒\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nSo $x=-1$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,0$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =3(-2+1)^{2}=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3(0+1)^{2}=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=-1.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=-1$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247419 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246615 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247419 1069 "q-113764" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247419 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247419 1071 "Given the function $f(x)={x}^{9}-9,$ which of the following statements are true?" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247419 1072 #uuid "f824f329-6450-55fc-a6ac-f26dadd16fc4" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247419 1074 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=9{x}^{8}.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n9{x}^{8}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =9(-1)^{8}=9>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =9(1)^{8}=9>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246799 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247420 1069 "q-113765" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247420 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247420 1071 "Given the function $f(x)=7-{x}^{7},$ which of the following statements are true?" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247420 1072 #uuid "0e451a2f-a172-5418-9012-79b7dfa6f624" 13194139533394 17592186045585 true] [17592186247420 1074 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-7{x}^{6}.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-7{x}^{6}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-7(-1)^{6}=-7<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-7(1)^{6}=-7<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." 13194139533394 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-000065eb-9a60-5f49-8f00-8086922a2524" 17592186244305 "answer-002a439c-2842-5d1e-810b-2b542db359a5" 17592186244306 "answer-003514cc-e435-5fac-a69f-9608a9ba982c" 17592186244307 "answer-003b3e3a-219a-5a63-82f9-e114c9b4d37e" 17592186244308 "answer-0042beb9-85ba-5f14-a38a-a5a956e608ba" 17592186244309 "answer-0058830f-70ca-5865-aa98-42fb87a57218" 17592186244310 "answer-005ed5ab-358e-5cee-bed6-73e25503b728" 17592186244311 "answer-00620c65-704d-5726-81c0-e1d595c4e2e9" 17592186244312 "answer-006394a5-7779-5635-a7a3-7eee92e00d07" 17592186244313 "answer-00715d81-4074-51f5-8298-3731ee240cb7" 17592186244314 "answer-0089083f-7266-5551-a84d-fa3924464d56" 17592186244315 "answer-009bc5f0-ba6c-57cc-95a0-70f6159a933f" 17592186244316 "answer-00a32f43-637b-5461-